  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "lesson-1",
      "name": "Pre-Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": true,
      "has_lesson_plan": false,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "assessment": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-1",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "building-data-driven-systems-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Intro to Data Structures",
      "name": "Lesson 1: Intro to Data Structures",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How does clear data organization improve what users and AI tools can understand and create?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how to organize related data using arrays, moving beyond single-value variables to structured collections. They construct arrays, retrieve values by index to update the DOM, and utilize properties like .length. The lesson emphasizes the importance of clear data structure for both user-facing features and AI comprehension, with students using AI to generate sample data and debug common indexing errors while maintaining responsibility for verifying the code.",
        "student_overview": "**How does clear data organization improve what users and AI tools can understand and create?**\n\nIn this lesson, you learn how to organize lists of related information using a data structure called an array. You build a personal favorites list and a dynamic magazine cover by creating arrays and writing code to display specific items on the screen based on their position. Along the way, you use AI to help generate content and fix bugs, learning why keeping data organized helps both you and computer programs understand your app better."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Intro to Data Structures",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "building-data-driven-systems-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Turning Data Into Displays",
      "name": "Lesson 2: Turning Data Into Displays",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How does defining a schema first make both interfaces and AI scaffolds clearer and safer?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn how defining an informal schema helps them structure data clearly and safely before rendering it in an interface. They brainstorm fields and data types, refine their schema using AI, and then update an app display to match their design. Students also consider privacy implications and evaluate how schema decisions affect clarity and accessibility.",
        "student_overview": "**How does defining a schema first make both interfaces and AI scaffolds clearer and safer?**\n\nIn this lesson, you learn how to plan out the structure of your data using a schema so your app displays information clearly and safely. You design and refine your own schema, get feedback from AI, and then update an app to match your plan. By the end, you see how good data planning makes interfaces easier to understand and helps protect users’ privacy."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Turning Data Into Displays",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "building-data-driven-systems-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Basic Array Methods",
      "name": "Lesson 3: Basic Array Methods",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can array methods help us manage collections reliably?**\n\nIn this lesson, students build intuition for managing dynamic data by applying basic array methods—push, pop, shift, and unshift—to real-world scenarios. Through a \"Social Media Story Scheduler\" activity, students act as digital planners, using AI to co-build helper functions that manage parallel arrays (*handling urgent posts or cancellations*). The lesson concludes with a debugging challenge where students identify \"desynced\" data, reinforcing the concept that when arrays hold related data, every method call must update all arrays to avoid mismatched information.",
        "student_overview": "**How can array methods help us manage collections reliably?**\n\nYou learn how to add, remove, and reorganize items in a list using code commands like push, pop, shift, and unshift. You apply these skills as a social media planner, building tools to handle real-life situations like posting an urgent humidity alert or removing a canceled event. Along the way, you use AI to explain how these commands work and help you debug your code when your data lists get out of sync."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Basic Array Methods",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "building-data-driven-systems-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Objects for Real-World Data",
      "name": "Lesson 4: Objects for Real-World Data",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can we represent real-world data fairly and clearly in code?**\n\nIn this lesson, students shift from using parallel arrays to representing real-world entities using JavaScript objects, creating a more cohesive data structure. Through a co-building activity with AI using the PAIR framework, students create, refactor, and update objects, learning to access properties via both dot and bracket notation. The lesson emphasizes the ethical implications of data representation, challenging students to consider how property choices (schema) shape meaning, fairness, and inclusivity.",
        "student_overview": "**How can we represent real-world data fairly and clearly in code?**\n\nYou learn how to model real-world items in code using JavaScript objects. You collaborate with AI to build and refine these objects, moving beyond simple lists to structured data that describes specific properties. You practice updating this information using different coding techniques and reflect on how the data fields you choose can impact fairness and how users experience an app."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Objects for Real-World Data",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "building-data-driven-systems-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lists of Objects",
      "name": "Lesson 5: Lists of Objects",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do our data modeling choices influence what users and AI can learn or miss?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how lists of objects help organize and manage data that programs and AI systems rely on. They will examine how incomplete or unbalanced lists can introduce **bias**, shaping the outcomes of programs built on those datasets. By the end of the lesson, students will understand both the technical skills for using lists and the importance of creating fair, representative datasets.",
        "student_overview": "**How do our data modeling choices influence what users and AI can learn or miss?**\n\nToday you learn how computers use lists of objects to store and organize information in a program. You also see how missing or unbalanced data in a list can lead to bias, which can affect how an AI system makes decisions. By the end, you know not just how to build and use lists, but how to make sure the datasets you create are fair and representative."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lists of Objects",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "building-data-driven-systems-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Rendering Lists of Objects",
      "name": "Lesson 6: Rendering Lists of Objects",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can we turn structured data into clear, useful visuals for users?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how to transform structured data into visible interface elements using the .map() method. Using the CHAT framework, they iteratively craft a reusable AI prompt to build a schema-to-card renderer for a community resource app. The lesson emphasizes tracing the flow of data from JavaScript objects to the DOM and refining AI interactions to ensure accurate, user-friendly displays.",
        "student_overview": "**How can we turn structured data into clear, useful visuals for users?**\n\nIn this lesson, you learn how to turn a list of raw data into a clean, visual display using the `.map()` method. You use the CHAT framework to guide AI in building a \"Cooling Center\" finder, refining your prompts to ensure your app processes and displays information correctly while learning to trace how data moves through your code."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Rendering Lists of Objects",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "building-data-driven-systems-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Evaluating Fairness and Accessibility",
      "name": "Lesson 7: Evaluating Fairness and Accessibility",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can the way we display data affect fairness, accessibility, and trust?**\n\nIn this lesson, students examine how the presentation of data impacts user experience, specifically regarding fairness and accessibility. Acting as accessibility consultants, they use AI to audit code for barriers—such as missing labels or unclear text—and analyze how design choices can unintentionally privilege certain perspectives or obscure others. Students then implement specific code revisions to ensure their applications are inclusive and accurate.",
        "preparation": "- (Optional) Print copies of the Accessible or Not? handout (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can the way we display data affect fairness, accessibility, and trust?**\n\nYou take on the role of an accessibility consultant to evaluate whether a data display is fair, clear, and usable for everyone. Using AI as an auditing partner, you spot design flaws—like hard-to-read text or biased layouts—and write code fixes to ensure that no user is left out or misled by the way data appears on the screen."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Evaluating Fairness and Accessibility",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "building-data-driven-systems-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Filtering",
      "name": "Lesson 8: Filtering",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can filter functions help users explore collections fairly and effectively?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn how filtering helps AI systems narrow down large sets of data to find information that is relevant to a specific goal. They explore examples of filtering in everyday technologies and practice applying filtering criteria to curated datasets. By the end of the lesson, students will understand how filters support efficient decision-making and how choosing different filters can change the output of a program.",
        "preparation": "- Gather several index cards or sticky notes",
        "student_overview": "**How can filter functions help users explore collections fairly and effectively?**\n\nToday you learn how computers use filters to sort through lots of information and keep only what matters for a task. You try using different filters yourself and see how they change the results you get. By the end, you understand why filtering is an important first step in helping systems make good decisions."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Filtering",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "building-data-driven-systems-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Searching",
      "name": "Lesson 9: Searching",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can search functions help users find what they need in a web app?**\n\nIn this lesson, students move beyond simple rendering to implementing interactive search functionality. They explore the nuances of string matching—identifying why queries like \"blue\" might fail against \"Blue\"—and establish rules for fair, predictable user experiences. Through a choice-based activity using the CHAT framework (Clarify, Hone, Apply, Transfer), students co-build a search feature with AI, focusing on handling edge cases, debugging logic, and validating their functions with rigorous testing.",
        "student_overview": "**How can search functions help users find what they need in a web app?**\n\nIn this lesson, you build a search feature that helps users find exactly what they are looking for. You learn how to handle tricky situations—like differences in capitalization or empty search boxes—and use the CHAT framework to collaborate with AI to build, debug, and test a robust search function for your own app."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Searching",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "building-data-driven-systems-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Sorting and Chaining",
      "name": "Lesson 10: Sorting and Chaining",
      "absolute_position": 11,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can sort and map functions shape user experience and trust in web apps?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how sorting helps programs organize information based on specific attributes or priorities. They then learn how “chaining” allows multiple steps—such as filtering, sorting, and selecting—to be combined to achieve more complex goals. By the end of the lesson, students will understand how chaining operations together enables programs to perform multi-step reasoning on structured data.",
        "student_overview": "**How can sort and map functions shape user experience and trust in web apps?**\n\nToday you learn how to **sort** data so it’s organized in useful ways, like ranking items from best to worst or shortest to longest. You also learn how to **chain** steps together—first filtering, then sorting, then choosing results—to solve more complicated problems. By the end, you see how chaining makes a program feel like it’s “thinking” through a multi-step plan."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Sorting and Chaining",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "building-data-driven-systems-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Value Explorer Project",
      "name": "Lesson 11: Value Explorer Project",
      "absolute_position": 12,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can we design, build, and refine a data-driven web app that is usable, inclusive, and trustworthy, while taking responsibility for our data choices and our use of AI scaffolds?**\n\nIn this project, students design a data-driven app that explores a value of personal importance through a schema, dataset, and interactive features. They examine how data and UI decisions shape user understanding, fairness, and trust. Students iteratively refine their work with AI support, peer feedback, and reflection.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Values Explorer Project Planning Guide to students (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can we design, build, and refine a data-driven web app that is usable, inclusive, and trustworthy, while taking responsibility for our data choices and our use of AI scaffolds?**\n\nIn this project, you design a data-driven app based on a value you care about and build a dataset, interface, and interactive features to explore it. You use reflection and iteration to make your app clear, fair, and useful for real users."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Value Explorer Project",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "building-data-driven-systems-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-2",
