  ],
  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "d1a4934b-08a4-4a11-8c91-6dddbaa079b8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Pre-Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d1a4934b-08a4-4a11-8c91-6dddbaa079b8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6cd468b9-90ec-458e-8a88-009bfcef4c58"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ae926563-d6fb-46f3-9d04-2044b86f4922",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ae926563-d6fb-46f3-9d04-2044b86f4922",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6cd468b9-90ec-458e-8a88-009bfcef4c58"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6928fc04-d569-43b5-b9ad-24488313e2a3",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students in pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair of students a copy of the Perfect Shelf activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students examine the sequence of items on their Perfect Shelf handout. They should look for thematic similarities and name the collection accordingly (e.g., \"stickies\" or \"markers\"). Point out the position numbering in the example shelf and have students number the items on their shelf. Have students determine the length (How many elements does it contain?), and compare the `length` to its last position (`length − 1`).",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Ask a series of call and response questions:\n\n- What item is at position 2?\n- What's the position of the daily planner?\n- What's the position of the pencil? (There are two!)\n\nThe goal is to quickly have students get used to associating elements with indices and vice versa. When given an index, it is easier to relate it to the correct element. When given the element and asked to retrieve the index, this can be trickier, especially when there are duplicate values (like the pencil). If they aren't given explicit directions, they might also start labelling indices at 1 instead of 0."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6928fc04-d569-43b5-b9ad-24488313e2a3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b5297da6-c8e8-4acc-a10a-c036ded3a8d0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "94052314-8d0c-4375-ad2f-b35dc9050df9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "94052314-8d0c-4375-ad2f-b35dc9050df9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b5297da6-c8e8-4acc-a10a-c036ded3a8d0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a3ec842a-547c-426e-a735-da5eed4955a0",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a3ec842a-547c-426e-a735-da5eed4955a0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b5297da6-c8e8-4acc-a10a-c036ded3a8d0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "46659150-d05c-4522-b1d5-3da66efb09d8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Give students a shared definition of arrays before they describe and use them.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *array*.\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Practice declaring an array, using indices, and using `.length` to power a simple user-facing page.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 1 to learn how to declare an array, use indices, and use .length. Remind students to keep their array items related (all songs, all foods, etc.), and to follow each TODO..\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that arrays have at least three string items and that IDs in `getElementById` match the HTML exactly. Watch for undefined on the page and ask: Which index are you using? How many items are in your array? Prompt students to use AI only for checking IDs, getting an example favorites array, or debugging a specific undefined error, and to read and understand any AI-suggested line before using it.",
        "progression_name": "Build Your Favorites",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If many students forget `favorites.length - 1` for the last item, quickly sketch a three-item array on the board with indices 0, 1, 2 and label `length` and `length - 1` before sending them back to code."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "46659150-d05c-4522-b1d5-3da66efb09d8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3f56e04-21b2-426b-8fbc-4a2a833a4851"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fde91660-accd-4347-8fdd-fa324875b3b3",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fde91660-accd-4347-8fdd-fa324875b3b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3f56e04-21b2-426b-8fbc-4a2a833a4851"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf1463eb-b3bd-4aaa-80a6-592efc293095",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** To address the common confusion between `.length` (count) and index (position), ensuring students understand why the last item is always `length - 1`.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students turn and talk with a partner to discuss the questions about arrays.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What is the length of this list? What is the index of \"Burger\"? Why are these numbers different?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should articulate that computers start counting at 0. Therefore, a list of 3 items has indices 0, 1, and 2. The length is always one number higher than the highest index.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students are struggling, refer back to the \"Perfect Shelf\" warm-up where they labeled the first item as 0. Remind them: \"Length is how many items fit in the box. Index is the sticker number on the item.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf1463eb-b3bd-4aaa-80a6-592efc293095",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3f56e04-21b2-426b-8fbc-4a2a833a4851"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a58b08c2-8af4-4846-aeff-c2301e6b3236",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Build Your Favorites"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a58b08c2-8af4-4846-aeff-c2301e6b3236",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3f56e04-21b2-426b-8fbc-4a2a833a4851"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2c44e50a-8356-4403-8818-d91cacf55868",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 2 to design a magazine cover using arrays. Make sure students understand that all three arrays must stay aligned—index 0 in each array belongs together, index 1 belongs together, and so on. Encourage them to keep arrays to 3–5 entries so they can focus on getting the index logic right.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ask students to show you where they declare `headlines`, `coverImages`, and `taglines`, and how they use the same index to populate `headline`, `coverImage.src`, and `tagline`. Check for mismatched lengths or broken images and prompt them to use AI only for specific pieces (like suggesting three sports headlines in an array) rather than full rewrites.",
        "progression_name": "Building Magazine Covers with Arrays",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle to see why arrays must stay aligned, have them describe what would happen to a user if the headline and image described totally different stories. Connect this back to clear data organization for better user experience."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2c44e50a-8356-4403-8818-d91cacf55868",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3f56e04-21b2-426b-8fbc-4a2a833a4851"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "21438d2d-bb9b-457f-817d-9289bcf6b288",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "21438d2d-bb9b-457f-817d-9289bcf6b288",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3f56e04-21b2-426b-8fbc-4a2a833a4851"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0ad7f010-91bb-4fa9-b88c-3404c44eab8a",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Pair students up. Have them review each other’s code and find where the index variable is created and referenced.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If you change the index variable (ex: `let index = 1`), how many things on the screen would change?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should point out that three things: the cover image, headline, and tagline should change. Listen for students to explain that the index variable is the bridge connecting the separate arrays that keeps the display items synchronized.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *For our magazine covers, if we delete the first title from the list but keep the first image, what happens to the magazine covers?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** This question immediately forces students to visualize the \"shift\" in data. It highlights that without a shared container (like an object, which comes later in Lesson 4 ), our program only connects items based on their position (index) in the list.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 3. Have students run the code first, then use the on-screen Debugging Checklist to inspect the index for the third item, the expression using `snacks.length + 1`, and the calculation for the last snack.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for students noticing undefined and counts that are off by one. Ask: How many items are in snacks? What are the valid indices? What does `snacks.length` equal here? Guide them toward changing the third index to 2, removing `+ 1` from the count, and using `snacks.length - 1` for the last snack. Encourage them to talk through each fix in words (what the code now does) before testing.",
        "progression_name": "Debugging Mini-Challenge",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "✅ Assessment Opportunity: Check that students can explain why snacks.length - 1 gives the last item and can connect each fix to what changes on the page."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0ad7f010-91bb-4fa9-b88c-3404c44eab8a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3f56e04-21b2-426b-8fbc-4a2a833a4851"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c48d63ac-b7ff-4743-93f3-832444c1114f",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c48d63ac-b7ff-4743-93f3-832444c1114f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3f56e04-21b2-426b-8fbc-4a2a833a4851"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "98738301-8501-43ea-ab20-0933c63c22b9",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students open Lesson 1, Level 4 and write a reflection that reflects on how AI assisted with their work with arrays (favorites, magazine covers, debugging).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Encourage specific references to their own code (playlists, headlines, snacks). Ask: How would your code look if you used separate variables instead of arrays? How might that confuse a user? How did clear arrays and names help AI give better suggestions or make debugging easier?",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "98738301-8501-43ea-ab20-0933c63c22b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3f56e04-21b2-426b-8fbc-4a2a833a4851"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "614506cc-ce8b-42e4-bbea-b4b72c65ff91",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Array",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students are stuck, offer sentence starters like: \"Arrays made it easier to...\" or \"If I did not use arrays, I would have to...\" but avoid giving full sample answers so reflections stay authentic."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "614506cc-ce8b-42e4-bbea-b4b72c65ff91",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3f56e04-21b2-426b-8fbc-4a2a833a4851"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b09f2317-fb44-48f1-ac1c-f5c591973eef",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b09f2317-fb44-48f1-ac1c-f5c591973eef",
        "lesson_activity.key": "55b85beb-9cbe-44a7-bc7d-03d4d7f38967"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c630529e-108b-4c2b-9ae0-350e9808f1df",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 7 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c630529e-108b-4c2b-9ae0-350e9808f1df",
        "lesson_activity.key": "55b85beb-9cbe-44a7-bc7d-03d4d7f38967"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "40c527fd-609a-4576-9969-b50d7e746705",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students notice that familiar apps use a consistent structure (schema) behind the scenes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What kinds of data are being displayed on this screen? How do you know they’re related?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface that each item (song, podcast, album) repeats the same set of fields in the same order, hinting that there is a consistent underlying structure.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Can you tell which data might be personal or private? How does the app keep that separate? If this data were shown to the wrong person, what could go wrong?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Emphasize that some fields (names, IDs) are more sensitive than others, and that apps must separate or hide these fields to protect users.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "40c527fd-609a-4576-9969-b50d7e746705",
        "lesson_activity.key": "595165f5-7f43-45f7-a1a2-ca6f18247505"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d39eca60-be54-4a8e-a97c-6231b3f0230c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *schema*.\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** To explicitly define schema and connect it to a commonly known structure (contact cards in phones)"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d39eca60-be54-4a8e-a97c-6231b3f0230c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1e5d691d-d57b-4fab-92d9-b12284dbe746"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "15595607-f933-449a-a460-666bfb3e1237",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Just like a house needs a blueprint before you build it, an app needs a schema before you code it. A schema tells the computer exactly what \"ingredients\" go into every item on your list. If you want to create a basic contact card, for instance, you might add name (a string variable), phone (a number variable), and a portrait or icon (an image)",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "15595607-f933-449a-a460-666bfb3e1237",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1e5d691d-d57b-4fab-92d9-b12284dbe746"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "af276208-65e2-4eef-95a4-b0c4bc9902ae",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 2, Level 1-3\n- Level 1: Students design a schema in Sketch Lab around a specific topic.\n- Level 2: Students utilize an AI chat to refine your schema.\n- Level 3: Students they will now turn their refined schemas into real interfaces\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are not using real personal data and are labeling fields with clear data types. Ask, “Which of these fields would you actually show on-screen?” Look for evidence that students working through these levels step by step. If they are running into bugs, ask them if they have updated the html and css according to their schema. Check that students are questioning AI suggestions rather than copying them. Ask, “Why did you accept or reject that AI suggestion?”",
        "progression_name": "Brainstorming a Schema "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "af276208-65e2-4eef-95a4-b0c4bc9902ae",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1e5d691d-d57b-4fab-92d9-b12284dbe746"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "99e7e7c9-160d-4d24-b45e-c3c5d625dcd6",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Iterate on Your Schema"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "99e7e7c9-160d-4d24-b45e-c3c5d625dcd6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1e5d691d-d57b-4fab-92d9-b12284dbe746"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a388f43e-23a6-43a2-9fc6-b0b3082811e4",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Display Your Schema",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students who finish early to help peers by asking them clarifying questions about their fields (for example, “Would every item have this field?”) rather than editing for them."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a388f43e-23a6-43a2-9fc6-b0b3082811e4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1e5d691d-d57b-4fab-92d9-b12284dbe746"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "382b4c1e-d892-40cf-aada-de0afb2d1619",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "382b4c1e-d892-40cf-aada-de0afb2d1619",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1e5d691d-d57b-4fab-92d9-b12284dbe746"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6b0d229e-96f8-4abd-827d-c023f92891fe",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6b0d229e-96f8-4abd-827d-c023f92891fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1e5d691d-d57b-4fab-92d9-b12284dbe746"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "054e9b14-55de-4945-97cc-5070e7515e82",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What choices did you make about which fields to show and how to lay them out on the screen?*\nHow do those choices help make the display clear and accessible for users? (You might mention labels, order, grouping, or readability.)\nHow did you protect users’ privacy or safety when deciding what not to show?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Listen for students to make connections between the schema they developed and how that thinking and planning influenced what or how information was displayed on their app screens.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Level 5. Explain that students will practice debugging a misaligned schema in a storefront. Once they have completed the debugging, they should move on to complete the two vocabulary levels on schema.\n- Level 5: Use the Human Debugging Checklist to identify symptoms on the storefront, predict causes like different array lengths or hard-coded indices, fix the code to follow a simple schema (name, price, image), and use a loop or .map to render products. Then consult Web Lab AI Tutor to compare approaches.\n- Level 6: Read the panel definition of schema and examine the example table.\n- Level 7: Write a free-response explanation of schema in their own words with an example.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** During debugging, watch for students aligning array lengths and replacing hard-coded indices with loops. Ask, \"What schema are these arrays trying to follow?\" and \"How can you make sure each index lines up across arrays?\"",
        "progression_name": "Safe and Clear Displays"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "054e9b14-55de-4945-97cc-5070e7515e82",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1e5d691d-d57b-4fab-92d9-b12284dbe746"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0753d03e-766f-4b93-b1b0-64088804e452",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Fix the Storefront Display"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0753d03e-766f-4b93-b1b0-64088804e452",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1e5d691d-d57b-4fab-92d9-b12284dbe746"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a80b13dc-02f5-4986-b951-b28bcc08f5ec",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Schema",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student explanations on Level 7 to check who understands schema well enough to move into design and coding, and who may need a quick small-group re-teach."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a80b13dc-02f5-4986-b951-b28bcc08f5ec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1e5d691d-d57b-4fab-92d9-b12284dbe746"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1f6fb638-2a5a-40c0-8af4-5669852aeff9",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1f6fb638-2a5a-40c0-8af4-5669852aeff9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1e5d691d-d57b-4fab-92d9-b12284dbe746"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "897fd4dc-47a8-4164-b5d7-9dd1f7e237bf",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What did AI suggest? How were its ideas similar to or different from yours?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Invite a few students to share how they fixed the storefront and how AI's suggested fix compared. Capture one or two sample strategies on the board (for example, \"create an array of product objects\" vs. \"loop over index with aligned arrays\"). Emphasize that students can and should form their own debugging hypotheses, then use AI to cross-check and refine their reasoning, rather than letting AI make all the decisions."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "897fd4dc-47a8-4164-b5d7-9dd1f7e237bf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1e5d691d-d57b-4fab-92d9-b12284dbe746"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "888d816d-f3b9-4b9f-9f41-a6163fb1b4f6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Facilitate a quick reflection using the Three W's.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "888d816d-f3b9-4b9f-9f41-a6163fb1b4f6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81f14130-771c-4802-8ce8-8d060c061951"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bdf92c79-1b5e-4d97-b435-dc566c9b0ec9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 8 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bdf92c79-1b5e-4d97-b435-dc566c9b0ec9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81f14130-771c-4802-8ce8-8d060c061951"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3c913076-92be-45eb-9a23-70a6892e6623",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Activate students' prior intuition about adding/removing items so they can connect it to array methods.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reintroduce the physical shelf of 4–5 items from Lesson 1. Remind them that this represents a collection, like an array. Have them share predictions for what they think each of the methods might do.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3c913076-92be-45eb-9a23-70a6892e6623",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ec7491e4-bb95-4fcf-acde-3d767c899ef5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0e02a2fe-5740-4aa7-a062-6ae9a65a685e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 2. Remind students to click each button multiple times, watch how size and order change, highlight event handlers and ask AI questions like \"Explain what this function does\" or \"What does push do to the array here?\"\n- Level 1: Explore the app with `push`, `pop`, `shift`, and `unshift` buttons and use AI as a code explainer.\n- Level 2: Reflect on what these array operations have in common and how they keep parallel arrays in sync.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students testing each button, checking that the display matches the array, and making small, safe edits. Ask probing questions: What changed in the array? Which method caused that change? How would you describe this in your own words? Support students who rely on AI to rewrite everything by redirecting them to ask explanation questions instead.",
        "progression_name": "Explore and Explain"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0e02a2fe-5740-4aa7-a062-6ae9a65a685e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cadafa67-9828-4997-9fd2-eaae435439ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "050a0503-5324-4ec5-91f4-abe1f752aeca",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Free Response: Array Operations",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student explanations on Level 2 to check whether they recognize that all four methods add or remove items at the ends of arrays and must be applied consistently across parallel arrays."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "050a0503-5324-4ec5-91f4-abe1f752aeca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cadafa67-9828-4997-9fd2-eaae435439ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7ec979a0-24e7-4395-a692-e2eae1f85605",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7ec979a0-24e7-4395-a692-e2eae1f85605",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cadafa67-9828-4997-9fd2-eaae435439ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6b1fa228-2027-4d65-857e-d5c4b85017d4",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What do `push`, `pop`, `shift`, and `unshift` all have in common?*\n- *How can helper functions keep multiple arrays in sync?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students articulate that these methods add or remove items at the ends of an array, changing size and sometimes order, and that to keep multiple arrays in sync they must use the same operation on every related array.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the PAIR Framework activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the PAIR framework explicitly and connect each step to today's helper-function work: Prompt AI for a helper, Analyze whether it uses the right method on all arrays, Integrate it into the helper section, and Reflect on how it protects data consistency.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep PAIR visible (on board or handout) so students can reference the steps while working through the Social Media Story Scheduler scenarios."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6b1fa228-2027-4d65-857e-d5c4b85017d4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cadafa67-9828-4997-9fd2-eaae435439ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c81e53e3-e571-44c4-ba51-1280bee10b22",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 3 to complete Levels 3 through 7. Remind students to follow PAIR for each helper: Prompt AI for a helper function using the correct array method, Analyze the different code implementations suggested by AI, Integrate one placing the helper in the correct section and calling it,and Reflect on what happens in the UI.\n- Level 3: Use the PAIR framework to create a helper function for `push()`\n- Level 4: Create a helper function for `pop()`\n- Level 5: Create a helper function for `unshift()`\n- Level 6: Create a helper function for `shift()`\n- Level 7: Reflect on how these helper functions protect data consistency in your project.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students keep the three arrays aligned, call helpers before `renderFeed()`, and avoid manual HTML edits. Ask: Which method are you using here? Did you update titles, contents, and images together? How can you test that everything stayed in sync?",
        "progression_name": "Social Media Story Scheduler"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c81e53e3-e571-44c4-ba51-1280bee10b22",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cadafa67-9828-4997-9fd2-eaae435439ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3f255e50-585e-4f53-97f3-c3effabc932d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Multiple Choice: Helper Functions",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Look at the finished social media feeds and Level 7 responses to see whether students can both implement and explain helpers that maintain parallel arrays."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3f255e50-585e-4f53-97f3-c3effabc932d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cadafa67-9828-4997-9fd2-eaae435439ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a664fba-ab70-48be-9a10-bf7f0af39e40",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a664fba-ab70-48be-9a10-bf7f0af39e40",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cadafa67-9828-4997-9fd2-eaae435439ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e3ed0a0-a285-4cd8-9cec-78effa8b03d5",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students respond to the reflection questions in their PAIR Framework activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 8. Before they start, remind students to focus on what breaks when only one array is updated and to connect that to today's idea of data consistency. Have students run the buggy app, observe when names, hours, and notes do not line up, and record observations with the Human Debugging Checklist.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ask students: When you add or remove a stop, what do you expect to see? What actually happened? Which helper looks suspicious? Encourage them to use precise terms like array method, `push`, `pop`, `shift`, and `unshift` in their observations and reflections.",
        "progression_name": "Debugging: Street Art Route Planner"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e3ed0a0-a285-4cd8-9cec-78effa8b03d5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cadafa67-9828-4997-9fd2-eaae435439ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f906c504-188b-46bd-8699-ca4b488d890b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f906c504-188b-46bd-8699-ca4b488d890b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20da547f-b2c5-49f2-bc9f-275787d04ad9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2f687057-c146-4043-8be0-b9b14736f0c8",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 9 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2f687057-c146-4043-8be0-b9b14736f0c8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20da547f-b2c5-49f2-bc9f-275787d04ad9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e4b9ac84-0562-4524-ba01-67c5f60bb1f7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Prepare students to brainstorm properties for a real-world item.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students in pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair of students a copy of the My First Object activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask pairs to choose one everyday item (suggest backpack, bicycle, or dessert) and write it on their template. Remind them to avoid personal or identity-based examples (like specific people) to keep the activity comfortable for everyone. Have pairs list three to four properties and example values for their chosen item on the template. Encourage them to think about what someone would need to know to understand or choose this item.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** If students are stuck, ask prompts like: What does it look like? How do you use it? Where would you see it? Keep examples concrete and non-sensitive.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students suggest using a person, gently redirect them toward non-person items and explain that you will talk about fairness and representation without using classmates or personal identities as examples."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e4b9ac84-0562-4524-ba01-67c5f60bb1f7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a52006ce-614a-46f0-acde-1426e7810f65"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a0190cf0-e181-46f9-a973-f9f5a3a06f7d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a0190cf0-e181-46f9-a973-f9f5a3a06f7d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a52006ce-614a-46f0-acde-1426e7810f65"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "871faea2-e117-4f8c-a52f-73cbda785b23",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What objects and properties did you come up with? How does each list change what the item seems like or who it is for?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that people bring different experiences and needs, so their property choices can differ. Emphasize that this is why fairness and clarity matter when we represent real-world data in code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "871faea2-e117-4f8c-a52f-73cbda785b23",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a52006ce-614a-46f0-acde-1426e7810f65"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e44f75ac-9f8d-4390-bedb-283ec99b4ee5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Bridge from the informal brainstorm to the formal coding idea of objects.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _object_. Explain that an object groups related pieces of information about one thing under a single name. Connect this to the brainstorm students just did — they listed details about an item, and in code, those details would be stored together as one object. Emphasize that how we choose to represent something shapes how understandable, fair, and useful it becomes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _property_. Point out that each label around the backpack represents one property — a named fact about the object. Explain that properties help the computer store and work with specific details, like color or size, and that these facts can be read or updated in code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 6. Walk students through the PAIR steps in the starter comments: Prompt the AI with their property list, Analyze what the AI added or changed, Integrate by editing the object and optionally adding a new property, and Reflect in code comments about one change and one thing they learned.\n- Level 1: Use the AI Tutor to generate an object from their warm-up properties, revise it for clarity and fairness, and display it on the page.\n- Level 2: Write about what the AI did well and what they changed or would change, focusing on their role as the human collaborator.\n- Level 3: Review the definition for _object_\n- Level 4: Write the definition for _object_ in their own words.\n- Level 5: Review the definition for _property_.\n- Level 6: Write the definition for _property_ in their own words.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for:\n- Objects with three to five properties using correct { key: value } syntax.\n- Students identifying and revising any unclear or biased properties or values from the AI.\n- Students successfully displaying the object via innerText or console.log.\n\nAsk probing questions like: What did the AI assume about this property? Does that feel fair or accurate? What did you decide to change and why?",
        "progression_name": "Create Your First Object"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e44f75ac-9f8d-4390-bedb-283ec99b4ee5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "151d79c5-d859-428b-bf36-5bcffec122fb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a165eaf0-8947-4e6d-9e46-e4e3bd5461fc",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Reflection: Human vs AI Object Design"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a165eaf0-8947-4e6d-9e46-e4e3bd5461fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "151d79c5-d859-428b-bf36-5bcffec122fb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5c67ac40-a135-487f-8ac4-e26778ac7ed9",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Object"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5c67ac40-a135-487f-8ac4-e26778ac7ed9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "151d79c5-d859-428b-bf36-5bcffec122fb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7375e3d2-c51b-4a18-8356-34276b3514e4",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Property",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that AI is a helper, not the boss. Encourage them to keep objects simple and neutral, and to write respectful comments when critiquing AI choices (for example, “The AI focused on X, but I changed it to Y because…”)."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use the reflection in Level 2 to check whether students understand both object structure and their ongoing responsibility when using AI to represent real-world data."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7375e3d2-c51b-4a18-8356-34276b3514e4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "151d79c5-d859-428b-bf36-5bcffec122fb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d6899bea-03c1-40fd-9b72-a2ea160eb06c",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d6899bea-03c1-40fd-9b72-a2ea160eb06c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "151d79c5-d859-428b-bf36-5bcffec122fb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c43b5e6-97dd-4670-bb9f-cb905e28a633",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Prepare students to refactor from parallel arrays to a single object and connect that object to the existing render function.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to describe the three parallel arrays and explain that each index represents one player, but the data is split across arrays. Then outline that they will create one object for the player instead of hard-coded placeholders."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c43b5e6-97dd-4670-bb9f-cb905e28a633",
        "lesson_activity.key": "151d79c5-d859-428b-bf36-5bcffec122fb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "124670f8-5cb6-4706-848d-4595a8338411",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Notice how the the player information is currently scattered across three arrays. We are going to group that into one object so it is easier to read and update.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "124670f8-5cb6-4706-848d-4595a8338411",
        "lesson_activity.key": "151d79c5-d859-428b-bf36-5bcffec122fb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c1c4d3c3-a46a-4dd4-b4ef-a285fe1b9f1c",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 7. Students create a dessert object and update the `render()` function to update the card.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for:\n- Correct object syntax with curly braces and key: value pairs.\n- Students updating the DOM using dot notation.\n- Students remembering to call `render()`.\n\nAsk questions like: How does this object make it easier to see all the Brownie data in one place? What would be harder if we kept using three arrays?",
        "progression_name": "Object from Arrays",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If you see students trying `dessert[0]`, remind them that arrays use numeric indexes but objects use named keys like dessert.name. Emphasize that this makes the code more readable."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1c4d3c3-a46a-4dd4-b4ef-a285fe1b9f1c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "151d79c5-d859-428b-bf36-5bcffec122fb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c12080b4-0ef9-482d-b909-e3482e11d6fe",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c12080b4-0ef9-482d-b909-e3482e11d6fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "151d79c5-d859-428b-bf36-5bcffec122fb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a73b84e6-a510-4e53-9f0e-c50f6a2ed364",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that dot notation is great when you know the property name ahead of time, while bracket notation is helpful when the property name comes from a variable or has special characters. Show the two short examples and connect them to the dessert object they just created."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a73b84e6-a510-4e53-9f0e-c50f6a2ed364",
        "lesson_activity.key": "151d79c5-d859-428b-bf36-5bcffec122fb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "40e010b0-e187-4ed5-8b0a-035bcfe57e89",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In the next level, you will use the AI to help you practice both dot notation and bracket notation with your dessert object.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "40e010b0-e187-4ed5-8b0a-035bcfe57e89",
        "lesson_activity.key": "151d79c5-d859-428b-bf36-5bcffec122fb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f86524d-44f8-4474-a770-5c5392844377",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 4, Level 8. Remind students to PROMPT the AI Tutor with questions like \"How do I update object properties using dot vs. bracket notation?\" and \"Why would I use bracket notation with a variable?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for:\n- Correct use of dot notation vs. bracket notation.\n- Buttons that actually trigger the functions (no console errors).\n- Students using the AI Tutor for targeted questions, not letting it write everything.\n\nAsk: Which syntax felt easier to you? Can you think of a case where you must use bracket notation?",
        "progression_name": "Dot Notation vs Bracket Notation",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students copy AI code that does not match the starter IDs or variable names, coach them to align the AI's suggestion with the provided code. Emphasize reading and understanding AI code before pasting."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f86524d-44f8-4474-a770-5c5392844377",
        "lesson_activity.key": "151d79c5-d859-428b-bf36-5bcffec122fb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e6a73e8-c634-4541-9bc3-8bb31ead2d15",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e6a73e8-c634-4541-9bc3-8bb31ead2d15",
        "lesson_activity.key": "151d79c5-d859-428b-bf36-5bcffec122fb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e3649333-1511-45fd-adab-1299c3865113",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 4, Level 9. Students should change at least two existing property values using dot notation or bracket notation, add at least one new property to the object, and modify the HTML and `render()` so all updated and new properties appear on screen.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for:\n- Objects that have clearly changed from the starter values and include a new property.\n- Matching IDs between HTML and JavaScript.\n- Students testing and verifying that the visual card reflects the updated data.\n\nAsk: How did changing the object data change what you see? What did you ask the AI Tutor to help you with, and how did you decide whether to accept its suggestion?",
        "progression_name": "Update and Evolve Objects",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students who finish early to add another small feature (for example, another property and line on the card) and to challenge themselves to write the necessary HTML/JS without AI first, then use AI to check or debug."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use students’ finished cards as a quick check that they can mutate objects, add properties, and connect data to the UI."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e3649333-1511-45fd-adab-1299c3865113",
        "lesson_activity.key": "151d79c5-d859-428b-bf36-5bcffec122fb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "49367105-4258-47f7-aa78-a9382d6dcc9a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *When we use AI to create objects, what responsibilities do we still have as the human collaborator?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students articulate that they must review AI outputs for clarity, fairness, and accuracy, make edits when needed, and take responsibility for the final code. Highlight examples from Level 2 reflections or today's objects where students changed AI suggestions to better represent the data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "49367105-4258-47f7-aa78-a9382d6dcc9a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "814172ea-6501-4ecf-bc84-c84f25e639f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "020bd8bd-69f5-41bb-8523-b1367186718a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 10 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "020bd8bd-69f5-41bb-8523-b1367186718a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "814172ea-6501-4ecf-bc84-c84f25e639f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0a8d3adb-0114-4eda-817e-1b08b8aed687",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Notice that missing or uneven data changes how information is represented.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What's one thing that seems missing or over-represented?*\n- *What’s one way an AI might get an unfair picture if it trained only on this data?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect patterns in the dataset (blanks, repeats, uneven groups) to how information can become unfair or incomplete. Listen for ideas like \"The dataset would ignore \" or \"An AI might think  is the only important group.\" Guide students to link representation in data to fairness.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0a8d3adb-0114-4eda-817e-1b08b8aed687",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7ed500d6-139f-43b0-800c-256394e86153"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8aabb5d8-f2cf-4115-a76a-1999981c54e3",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Connect arrays of objects to the idea of a dataset we can query and update.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to explain that each object in the array is like one row in a table (one item in the dataset), and the array is the full dataset. Point out that today's coding levels will use this structure to both explore clean data and notice missing data."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8aabb5d8-f2cf-4115-a76a-1999981c54e3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6f6e14b-3410-4867-8daf-91dac51ece0e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b17c55c-0958-4e15-9467-300bf888309e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We will first practice with a clean dataset, then with a messy one, so we can see how the same code behaves when information is missing.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b17c55c-0958-4e15-9467-300bf888309e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6f6e14b-3410-4867-8daf-91dac51ece0e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9cc128e7-ad32-456e-99dd-bc4965515653",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 and 2.\n- Level 1: Use the AI to generate an array of at least five school supply objects and paste it into the supplies variable. Then complete `getItemByIndex` and `updateLocationByIndex`, and test with valid and invalid indexes.\n- Level 2: Use `getItemByIndex`, and then AI-assisted `getItemById` and `getItemByName`, to inspect objects that have missing properties and see how that shows up in the UI.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that:\n- Students' supplies arrays in Level 1 have all four properties: id, name, location, isAvailable.\n- getItemByIndex uses `supplies[index]` and handles invalid indexes safely.\n- `updateLocationByIndex` first calls `getItemByIndex` and updates only `item.location` when item exists.\n- In Level 2, students actually test items with missing fields (IDs 3 or 4) and notice undefined or blanks.\n\nAsk probing questions like, \"What happens in the UI when a property is missing?\" and \"If this dataset were about people, who would be invisible to the AI?\"",
        "progression_name": "Lists of Objects",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If many students think the whole array is \"one big object,\" have them point with a finger to one { ... } object and label it as a single record, then trace the surrounding [] as the full dataset."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9cc128e7-ad32-456e-99dd-bc4965515653",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6f6e14b-3410-4867-8daf-91dac51ece0e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "883b64d6-9b24-4e4c-b445-83a5ac5465d3",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "883b64d6-9b24-4e4c-b445-83a5ac5465d3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6f6e14b-3410-4867-8daf-91dac51ece0e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "86c5990e-60c6-41e7-997f-59b8d13ebd18",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How did using helper functions like `getItemByIndex` or `getItemById` help you see where the data was clean or messy?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that helper functions act like \"lenses\" into a dataset, revealing both complete and missing information. Listen for comments like \"When I changed the index, I could see which objects were missing a name\" or \"Using `getItemById(3)` showed me the missing category.\" Steer the conversation toward how these coding tools help them diagnose bias or gaps.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define bias.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 5, Level 3. Remind them to start by prompting the AI Tutor to generate their dataset.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that:\n- Arrays (animals, events, or stops) have at least three objects and follow the suggested schema.\n- `yesNo` returns exactly \"Yes\" or \"No\" for true/false.\n- `getItemByIndex` simply returns `array[index]` for the chosen context.\n- `updateFieldByIndex` only changes the intended field on one object instead of reassigning the whole array.\n- addNewItem uses .push(newItem) and the count updates when `addBtn` is clicked.\n- `renderItem` use data from the array, not hard-coded strings.\n\nAsk questions like, \"Where could missing or uneven data show up in this UI?\" or \"If you left some fields blank, what might a user or AI misunderstand?\"",
        "progression_name": "Plan and Build",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students overwrite the entire array inside updateFieldByIndex, pause them to predict what the UI will show and then refactor together so only `array[index][fieldName]` changes."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "86c5990e-60c6-41e7-997f-59b8d13ebd18",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6f6e14b-3410-4867-8daf-91dac51ece0e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9bd461c4-443b-47c0-b70a-27624d7ad807",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9bd461c4-443b-47c0-b70a-27624d7ad807",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6f6e14b-3410-4867-8daf-91dac51ece0e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "231482d2-d5af-4bc6-8136-445ead7a787d",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students together based on the choice they made for Level 3: Animal Adoption, Community Events, or Metro Expansion.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Before teaching this lesson, think ahead about how students will physically move into groups. Consider the number of students in your class and the layout of your room. For example, if space is tight, designate clear group zones ahead of time or move furniture slightly to make movement easier. If you expect uneven group sizes, you might also plan how to support very small or very large groups so everyone can participate meaningfully."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "231482d2-d5af-4bc6-8136-445ead7a787d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6f6e14b-3410-4867-8daf-91dac51ece0e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2d1fa789-eeff-4e80-83ab-0d3b6a0428ed",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2d1fa789-eeff-4e80-83ab-0d3b6a0428ed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6f6e14b-3410-4867-8daf-91dac51ece0e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9fc0a736-3abb-4b8c-ac0b-24406f1317e0",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 4 to complete Levels 4 through 6.\n- Level 4: Respond to the Check Your Understanding question.\n- Level 5: Review the definition for _bias_.\n- Level 6: Explain the term _bias_ in their own words.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Encourage students to reference at least one helper function (for example, `getItemByIndex`, `updateFieldByIndex`, `addNewItem`, or `renderItem)` and at least one concrete detail from their dataset (like missing groups or repeated types). Ask, \"What would an AI think the world looks like based only on your data?\" to deepen their answers.",
        "progression_name": "Free Response: What AI Sees Shaped By Data Choices"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9fc0a736-3abb-4b8c-ac0b-24406f1317e0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6f6e14b-3410-4867-8daf-91dac51ece0e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "92cb0e65-c528-4e10-b722-7067c0bef029",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Bias",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student responses as an exit ticket to check who can clearly connect data choices and function behavior to biased or fair outcomes."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "92cb0e65-c528-4e10-b722-7067c0bef029",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6f6e14b-3410-4867-8daf-91dac51ece0e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "683a5942-8868-4856-aca5-3dbc7e0b63eb",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How do the choices we make when building a dataset affect what an AI can see or display?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students tie together their coding work, the warm-up, and the vocabulary term bias. Listen for explanations that mention schemas, missing fields, and which records were included or left out. Highlight comments that show how helper functions and UI made some patterns visible and others invisible.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "683a5942-8868-4856-aca5-3dbc7e0b63eb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "da1801c0-f329-4bf8-a151-8b2a5a59c141"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6ef9d72-7d32-44e4-ab0e-3b04aa9d766c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 7 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6ef9d72-7d32-44e4-ab0e-3b04aa9d766c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "da1801c0-f329-4bf8-a151-8b2a5a59c141"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4f999da1-0a3d-4dcf-96b7-58e91cb86a0d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students notice that many apps systematically transform data from one form to another.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students get into a small group (three to five students). The group should select one of the data transformation examples presented here :\n- A camera filter that turns every photo black-and-white.\n- A playlist app that adds a heart icon to every liked song.\n- A delivery app that converts street names into map pins.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What kind of hidden process occurs to transform the original data into these new displays?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to the idea that each input item goes through the same rule or process to create a new version (for example, every pixel becomes grayscale, every liked song gets a heart icon, every address turns into a pin). Emphasize that the original data often stays stored while a new version is created for display.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use a timer or have students pass a talking object to keep the flow moving. Works well to build quick consensus or identify patterns in responses."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4f999da1-0a3d-4dcf-96b7-58e91cb86a0d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a64a9a4-6c05-4da4-ab3f-5793eee77567"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f1c823f-e42e-4163-9df5-377845203c7a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f1c823f-e42e-4163-9df5-377845203c7a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a64a9a4-6c05-4da4-ab3f-5793eee77567"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "57d8ffb6-c429-4fcf-bfd3-058b4626aa3b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "57d8ffb6-c429-4fcf-bfd3-058b4626aa3b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a64a9a4-6c05-4da4-ab3f-5793eee77567"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a46de8e2-7c25-450f-87b8-9b26f4473772",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** To translate the \"filter\" analogy from the Warm Up into specific JavaScript syntax before students see it in the code editor."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a46de8e2-7c25-450f-87b8-9b26f4473772",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ef43b4c4-f918-4c47-ae92-ec7d8f8c2807",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In our warm-up, we talked about filters changing photos or apps changing addresses into pins. In JavaScript, the tool we use for this is called `.map()`. Think of `.map()` like a factory machine. You feed it a list of raw data. Inside the machine, a specific rule is applied to every single item. Out the other side comes a brand new list.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ef43b4c4-f918-4c47-ae92-ec7d8f8c2807",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cc207782-028b-4e9a-9c6c-e0cbc65b64f1",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the `.map()` method.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that the original list is not destroyed. This is crucial for data integrity—we don't want to lose our original data just because we want to display it differently."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cc207782-028b-4e9a-9c6c-e0cbc65b64f1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1aa0039e-8f19-4d4c-8761-3320149b7b3f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1aa0039e-8f19-4d4c-8761-3320149b7b3f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "08c743ad-790e-423b-a1b8-7c7d19943400",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 3.\n- Level 1: Run the given `nums.map(x => x * 2)` example, read the console output, and answer the comment questions about the rule and whether nums changed.\n- Level 2: Use `.map()` to create `supplyNames`, render the names, and optionally try the labeled card extension.\n- Level 3: Write a brief explanation of what `.map()` does.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students correctly identifying the \"rule\" in the number example (multiply each number by two) and noticing that the original array does not change. On the objects level, check that they are using `.map()` to pull out name and using .join(\"\") to build HTML without commas. Ask probing questions like, \"What stayed the same between the original array and the new one?\" and \"Where do you see the rule in your code?\" Redirect students who try to rewrite the starter code instead of focusing on the comments or who try to use loops instead of `.map()`.",
        "progression_name": "Introduction to .map() "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "08c743ad-790e-423b-a1b8-7c7d19943400",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4b5f77f5-a9ce-47f0-9a49-6c53e8f0ac57",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Free Response: Explain .map()"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4b5f77f5-a9ce-47f0-9a49-6c53e8f0ac57",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d2bf3c96-841a-4c30-aff1-6edf8a47955a",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d2bf3c96-841a-4c30-aff1-6edf8a47955a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fcbbdc81-cc89-4d44-9dbd-5521b4208f51",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We don't just \"put data on the screen.\" It has to go through the process illustrated here. You will build this exact process in the next level.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fcbbdc81-cc89-4d44-9dbd-5521b4208f51",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "51c14bfc-3520-44d1-a070-14bb6ce78b98",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the process of rendering objects as cards in a web app.\n- Data: Create an array of objects. Determine which data should be displayed and how.\n- Transform: Use `.map()` to extract object data and put into an HTML card container as a string\n- Combine: Use `.join(\"\")` to combine all of the HTML card container strings together.\n- Render: Insert combined HTML code into the DOM.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Based on your experience, where do you think this process could be useful in real apps?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface examples like changing formats (numbers to percentages), extracting one field from many objects (names, titles), or building visual elements from data (cards, list items). Emphasize that `.map()` lets us keep the original data intact while we create new views or displays, which connects directly to fairness and accessibility choices in how data is shown.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the CHAT framework.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "CHAT is a framework that students will use throughout the course. It is helpful for decomposing a larger problem into smaller steps, then creating targeted and focused prompts to implement one feature at a time. Once applied and tested, students will abstract a general reusable prompt template that they can reuse in future tasks. They will save this prompt into the backpack."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "51c14bfc-3520-44d1-a070-14bb6ce78b98",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9f71a5f1-90ba-43b3-9f1a-53fe81a60a1b",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9f71a5f1-90ba-43b3-9f1a-53fe81a60a1b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "500a20e3-526b-4f37-81ef-bb418fb9c457",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** To introduce and practice the CHAT framework.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reiterate that to build a complex app, we must decompose it down into smaller, manageable features or steps."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "500a20e3-526b-4f37-81ef-bb418fb9c457",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "04d69ac5-67a9-43b0-add2-4cf57332e320",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "04d69ac5-67a9-43b0-add2-4cf57332e320",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a32771cc-8255-43c2-91ce-2a4db368ef53",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In the \"Clarify\" step, you act as an Architect. You don't just say \"Build the app.\" You practice decomposition: breaking the big problem into smaller problems.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a32771cc-8255-43c2-91ce-2a4db368ef53",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7ad8e8ea-7e6e-41be-b746-c993da4f555f",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 6, Level 4. Students decompose design decisions and important features to implement for this app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for evidence that students are drafting prompts in the markdown files before asking AI for code. Support students in explicitly naming the steps, like \"I need to map the data to HTML strings\" rather than just guessing.",
        "progression_name": "Using CHAT to Generate System Prompts"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7ad8e8ea-7e6e-41be-b746-c993da4f555f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7b0935cc-496a-4259-843b-9a544791ef38",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7b0935cc-496a-4259-843b-9a544791ef38",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "31f84eef-a116-4c1a-9ce6-365b9b80a3e2",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What were some of the features or steps that you identified in the Clarify stage of CHAT?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should share concrete examples like \"I need a text label that says 'Open' or 'Closed'\" or \"I need a way to show the number of beds available.\" If they didn't write down \"I need to display the address,\" the AI likely won't do it. Decomposition means finding every single feature you want to appear on the screen and listing it before you start building.  Features and and steps to listen for:\n- Data Features (The \"What\"): Displaying the Name of the center. Showing the Capacity (number of people) or Status (Open/Closed). Creating a specific visual layout\n- Logic Steps (The \"How\"): Creating a function (like `makeCard`) to handle just one object. Using `.map()` to transform the entire list of data into HTML. Using `.join(\"\")` to Combine the list into a string without commas."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "31f84eef-a116-4c1a-9ce6-365b9b80a3e2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eb1d8708-70e6-4ef5-81d5-b7acbef84fb7",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In the \"Clarify\" step, you broke the app down into pieces. In \"Hone & Apply,\" you build those pieces one by one.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eb1d8708-70e6-4ef5-81d5-b7acbef84fb7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aa6feeb1-6827-4540-bf0d-dc130fcca0f2",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the Hone and Apply step of CHAT.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Based on how many of the decomposed features a student wants to implement into their app, they may go through many iterations of the Hone and Apply cycle. The goal is to focus on one feature at a time."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aa6feeb1-6827-4540-bf0d-dc130fcca0f2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7d5d5fd5-6ce6-47c5-ad5e-d7e4795ae7ed",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7d5d5fd5-6ce6-47c5-ad5e-d7e4795ae7ed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "95fc4db0-e7d3-44ad-af6b-e26db5470113",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 4 to complete Levels 5 and 6.\n- Level 5: Students draft a prompt that will create cards that will display the information in the centers dataset. Apply and test the code that AI produces.\n- Level 6: Choose an additional feature and write a draft for the specific feature. Apply and test the code that AI produces.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Use the Hone and Apply checklist to assist students who might be struggling:\n- Hone: Pick one feature from your Clarify list (e.g., \"Just render the names\"). Draft a specific prompt for it.\n- Apply: Run the code with real data. Does it work?\n- Repeat: Once it works, add the next feature (e.g., \"Add the capacity\" or \"Filter closed centers\").",
        "progression_name": "From Features to Code"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "95fc4db0-e7d3-44ad-af6b-e26db5470113",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e208c3a5-9d74-4fa0-a066-5342a57b183a",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What changes or revisions did you have to make to your prompts as you honed and applied them in your design?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface examples of iterative refinement where the AI's output wasn't perfect. Look for students mentioning: formatting fixes, UI tweaks, and increased specificity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Tell students that they will come up with a general \"Card Renderer\" prompt based on their experience from creating their Warming Center prompts."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e208c3a5-9d74-4fa0-a066-5342a57b183a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8d9dd9a7-4e6d-4f39-979f-ac1215a9e758",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You have built a great prompt that renders cards for Warming Centers. But a great engineer doesn't want to write that prompt again from scratch next time they build a Playlist app or a Menu app. We are going to use Transfer. I want you to look at your prompt and treat it like a \"Mad Libs\" game. Cross out every word that is specific to this app (like \"warming center\" or \"address\") and replace it with a general word (like \"item\" or \"text\"). You will do this in the prompt file.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle with the abstraction, encourage them to ask the AI to \"explain this code in one sentence\". Often, the AI's explanation will already be abstract (e.g., \"This function takes an object and maps it...\"), which students can use as inspiration for their generalized prompt."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8d9dd9a7-4e6d-4f39-979f-ac1215a9e758",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "90058b46-0dd0-4153-a7dc-fd949dd38ccf",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "90058b46-0dd0-4153-a7dc-fd949dd38ccf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "39fbdb37-1542-4eca-8808-e3c6e8bf40a2",
      "position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 6, Level 4. Students use abstraction to turn their specific \"Warming Center\" prompt into a general tool they can reuse in other contexts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** If students are struggling to abstract a prompt, you can share the following steps:\n1. Identify Specifics: Find words like \"Center,\" \"Capacity,\" or \"Address.\"\n1. Replace with General Terms: Change them to \"Item,\" \"Subtitle,\" or \"Property.\"\n1. Save: Save this general prompt to your Backpack.",
        "progression_name": "Creating Reusable Prompts"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "39fbdb37-1542-4eca-8808-e3c6e8bf40a2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2de0b727-f8c9-480b-b4c6-12ece706a182"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c5a1fd79-d552-4ad9-97fe-b3c79a59cee2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c5a1fd79-d552-4ad9-97fe-b3c79a59cee2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "75c7fb7d-fc44-4820-ab2b-3a521731e4ea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c180db25-1b5d-45c3-8af4-47df900e4a9f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 8 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c180db25-1b5d-45c3-8af4-47df900e4a9f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "75c7fb7d-fc44-4820-ab2b-3a521731e4ea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b4d1ef3-98b5-4e9c-8f1f-5f0028049362",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Get students noticing how small display choices change clarity, accessibility, and fairness.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the slides with Display A and Display B. Set a one-minute timer for each and have students silently observe both displays, looking for what is clear, confusing, or possibly unfair.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students finish early, prompt them to zoom in on tiny details like placeholder text, warning messages, or missing labels so they recognize subtle accessibility issues. Note that there is an optional handout that you can print or which students can access digitally it might be difficult for them to pick out details in slides alone."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b4d1ef3-98b5-4e9c-8f1f-5f0028049362",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53422d94-35ce-47c4-90a3-ee43187ff8b1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "429ee9f9-ed16-4a7a-b6c0-c13058ced97f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "429ee9f9-ed16-4a7a-b6c0-c13058ced97f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53422d94-35ce-47c4-90a3-ee43187ff8b1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9d974a50-1183-468d-9f07-29de49e5d730",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students to discuss the issues that they see in Display A.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students to discuss the issues that they see in Display B.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students finish early, prompt them to zoom in on tiny details like placeholder text, warning messages, or missing labels so they recognize subtle accessibility issues."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9d974a50-1183-468d-9f07-29de49e5d730",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53422d94-35ce-47c4-90a3-ee43187ff8b1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "40b9f091-365e-41ea-a229-9e3e7ca84dc2",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "40b9f091-365e-41ea-a229-9e3e7ca84dc2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53422d94-35ce-47c4-90a3-ee43187ff8b1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "67b08400-f627-49e4-83f6-b8791ba50d18",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Which display would you trust more, and why?*\n- *What patterns do you notice in which design we trust more?*\n- *What is one clue that helped you decide?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should name specific design details (labels, contrast, warnings, wording) that influence their trust and sense of fairness, not just say “A looks nicer” or “B looks better.” Help them connect these details to real users trying to understand information. Surface that clearer labels, readable text, and more inclusive wording lead to greater trust and perceived fairness, setting up the idea that display choices can include or exclude people.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Possible issues that students might point out include the following:\n\n- The Visibility Error (CSS): In Display A, the display uses a yellow text color on a white background. This fails accessibility standards because there isn't enough contrast. Display B uses dark grey text (#222) and a blue border. This makes it readable for everyone\n- The Logic Error (Bias): Display A creates a list that seems to say \"If this course isn't STEM, it doesn't belong in the Recommended list.\" This is algorithmic bias. In Display B, this filter is removed. The data speaks for itself without hiding options.\n- The Language Error (Inclusivity): Display A explicitly labels STEM as \"Elite\" and everything else as \"Basic.\" That’s a value judgment hard-coded into the app. Display B removes those labels entirely and just displays the Course Name and Type neutrally.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "67b08400-f627-49e4-83f6-b8791ba50d18",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53422d94-35ce-47c4-90a3-ee43187ff8b1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a2479098-4c73-41fb-bd7f-67a7cc100f3b",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a2479098-4c73-41fb-bd7f-67a7cc100f3b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53422d94-35ce-47c4-90a3-ee43187ff8b1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fa217fe5-b792-4c6b-b28a-df9b3bca66a8",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fa217fe5-b792-4c6b-b28a-df9b3bca66a8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53422d94-35ce-47c4-90a3-ee43187ff8b1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7d7d3f0d-13a4-4901-ae8d-b9f50dd48d83",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Make sure students share a common understanding of what accessibility means in the context of displays.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce examples of common accessibility issues (low contrast text, missing alt text, unlabeled forms, empty links/buttons).",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "These accessibility issues should serve as a brief review to concepts that students have already learned in Unit 1 and 2."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7d7d3f0d-13a4-4901-ae8d-b9f50dd48d83",
        "lesson_activity.key": "498e9f9a-f9b1-46ad-8bd4-94435c866c92"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0803c4c9-31f0-4ca1-9614-c3ed0db8470f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0803c4c9-31f0-4ca1-9614-c3ed0db8470f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "498e9f9a-f9b1-46ad-8bd4-94435c866c92"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "56e2d9b0-2dd4-4393-bc41-18bfa2be9031",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly define the three terms, then facilitate a quick partner discussion.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which one do you think is the hardest for a computer to judge automatically? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that while computers can easily check contrast ratios (Accessibility) or overflow boundaries (Readability), judging \"Clarity\" (how easy a sentence is to understand for a human) is more subjective and requires advanced AI processing. This sets up the need for the PAIR method in the next level."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "56e2d9b0-2dd4-4393-bc41-18bfa2be9031",
        "lesson_activity.key": "498e9f9a-f9b1-46ad-8bd4-94435c866c92"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c74c173c-22b8-4a0f-a9b6-366b58808c63",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this activity, think of the AI as your intern. It can point out possible problems, but you are the reviewer who decides what really makes the page more fair and accessible.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c74c173c-22b8-4a0f-a9b6-366b58808c63",
        "lesson_activity.key": "498e9f9a-f9b1-46ad-8bd4-94435c866c92"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4cba685b-8b3f-4b36-b4c4-5fa895c457c7",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 and 2.\n- Level 1: Use the AI to review the code for accessibility and fairness issues, then accept or reject 2–3 suggestions and log your decisions in the reflection table.\n- Level 2: Use toggles and the AI to audit clarity, readability, and accessibility, then rewrite the summary text to be shorter, clearer, and accurate.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who:\n- Ask the suggested review prompt to AI and do not just copy everything it says.\n- Make small, safe edits (labels, alt text, contrast, simple ARIA attributes) without breaking the page.\n- Fill out the reflection table with specific suggestions and reasons for accepting or rejecting them.\n\nOn Level 2, check that they:\n- Use both Large Text and High Contrast toggles and record one clarity, one readability, and one accessibility issue.\n- Ask AI to rewrite the summary with specific constraints (word limit and correct numbers) and verify the AI did not change the counts.",
        "progression_name": "PAIR Accessibility Reviewer"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4cba685b-8b3f-4b36-b4c4-5fa895c457c7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "498e9f9a-f9b1-46ad-8bd4-94435c866c92"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dade1223-3fab-48dc-9d75-71c9b4123222",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Clarity, Readability & Accessibility",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students seem overwhelmed by AI output, tell them to highlight only two concrete suggestions they fully understand. It is better to do a few thoughtful changes than many risky ones."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "As you glance at reflection tables and updated summaries, look for students who can clearly justify why they accepted or rejected AI suggestions and who keep the summary both accurate and under the word limit."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dade1223-3fab-48dc-9d75-71c9b4123222",
        "lesson_activity.key": "498e9f9a-f9b1-46ad-8bd4-94435c866c92"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b9325d5b-6a5e-412c-9763-e4addf894c57",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b9325d5b-6a5e-412c-9763-e4addf894c57",
        "lesson_activity.key": "498e9f9a-f9b1-46ad-8bd4-94435c866c92"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c2f4f5d8-60e8-41b3-b77f-d1d6530c7756",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What was one AI suggestion you accepted?*\n- *What was one suggestion you rejected, and why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that AI can surface useful ideas but sometimes suggests unnecessary, incorrect, or overly complex changes. Encourage them to mention checking accuracy, thinking about real users, and avoiding changes they do not understand.\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students connect ordering and visibility in a feed to bias and perspective, then practice using AI to make a small fairness-focused change.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that in the next level, they will look for patterns like one category dominating the top slots or certain groups rarely appearing, and then use AI and code to adjust which activity appears as the “Top Pick.”"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c2f4f5d8-60e8-41b3-b77f-d1d6530c7756",
        "lesson_activity.key": "498e9f9a-f9b1-46ad-8bd4-94435c866c92"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5b180b0f-2917-4e8f-b920-60ea44c809de",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Imagine a real school board where the top three spots are always the same type of activity. Even if that was not the goal, what message does that send about who matters most? We are going to experiment with that idea in code.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5b180b0f-2917-4e8f-b920-60ea44c809de",
        "lesson_activity.key": "498e9f9a-f9b1-46ad-8bd4-94435c866c92"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5374732b-05c6-4cf4-a42d-8e4212ad7bbe",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Evaluating Fairness and Accessibility activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Levels 3-4.\n- Level 3: Students analyze which activities appear in the featured list and use PAIR to adjust the dataset order to address a fairness or perspective issue.\n- Level 4: Students review the text and properties inside each activity and revise them to improve clarity, inclusivity, and accuracy.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who are examining the activities array rather than editing HTML and using PAIR to ask focused questions about reordering or rewriting data. Listen for students identifying fairness issues (like category imbalance or missing images) and explaining how their change improves representation. Redirect students if they assume fairness means simply \"equal numbers\" or if they overlook the impact of unclear or exclusive wording.",
        "progression_name": "Bias and Fairness",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Levels 3 and 4 work best when students move directly from identifying issues in the Activity Guide to making small, targeted changes in the dataset or wording. If students are stuck, guide them back to a specific question in the Activity Guide rather than suggesting a fix. Encourage concise, focused PAIR prompts to keep iteration manageable."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "The activity guide responses and students' update code in level 3-4 can be used to formatively assess student understand of the lesson objectives."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5374732b-05c6-4cf4-a42d-8e4212ad7bbe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "498e9f9a-f9b1-46ad-8bd4-94435c866c92"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "177807cd-d55e-4a3a-8e2c-1c77727f898e",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "177807cd-d55e-4a3a-8e2c-1c77727f898e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "498e9f9a-f9b1-46ad-8bd4-94435c866c92"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "16d8ef6b-db0c-4b71-9503-8bd9ba00faa1",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students connect their specific coding changes to the bigger idea of fairness, accessibility, and trust in data displays.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What was one way today's displays affected fairness, accessibility, or trust?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students connect specific edits (like reordering items, revising text, addressing missing images, or improving labels) to the larger themes of fairness, accessibility, and trust in data displays. Emphasize that even small changes can meaningfully shift who is included or sidelined.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If conversation lags or students stay too general, follow up with: “Which AI suggestion or code change most improved clarity or fairness today?” Encourage students to name the specific change they made and why it improved the display. If needed, prompt them to revisit their Activity Guide or preview their updated display to ground their answer in evidence."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "16d8ef6b-db0c-4b71-9503-8bd9ba00faa1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "498e9f9a-f9b1-46ad-8bd4-94435c866c92"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4909bc44-f45d-4e31-b712-4d658ded640f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4909bc44-f45d-4e31-b712-4d658ded640f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ebd52f0-4b9f-4570-83e5-66009d657b18"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e8385d26-e645-49e7-918d-f5574384e02c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 5 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e8385d26-e645-49e7-918d-f5574384e02c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ebd52f0-4b9f-4570-83e5-66009d657b18"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "401185f5-674c-45f4-9971-5385daf7d292",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Recognize that filters are conditions that determine visibility, and that every filter rule carries consequence.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What kinds of artists do you think show up here? Who might be missing from this playlist?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** This warm up sets the stage for noticing patterns in a seemingly simple playlist and questioning who gets represented in recommendation systems. Students surface their assumptions about genre, popularity, and identity, then consider who is absent and why that absence matters. The goal is to spark curiosity about how filtering choices shape what we see.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle, prompt with questions like “Do you see any indie or older artists?” or “What do these artists have in common?” to steer them toward visibility and omission, not musical taste."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "401185f5-674c-45f4-9971-5385daf7d292",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d78f02e1-22ad-4bce-8bc4-24affaa9cbdd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8776b713-afe6-439b-bb51-725ca13d91f8",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8776b713-afe6-439b-bb51-725ca13d91f8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d78f02e1-22ad-4bce-8bc4-24affaa9cbdd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8cdf2e1b-bc4d-47a8-8a51-692816269a43",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How is this playlist different from the previous one? Who might feel more included here?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** This discussion helps students compare two curated lists so they can see how subtle shifts in filtering change who appears and who feels represented. By noticing differences in genre, style, or artist identity, students start to connect filtering choices with inclusion. The goal is to build awareness that filters are design decisions that shape user experience.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep attention on patterns (variety of genres) rather than whether one playlist is “better.” Redirect any moral debates back to what is visible vs. invisible."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8cdf2e1b-bc4d-47a8-8a51-692816269a43",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d78f02e1-22ad-4bce-8bc4-24affaa9cbdd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "caaf06f3-1146-4b76-bf41-5312899dd853",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "caaf06f3-1146-4b76-bf41-5312899dd853",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d78f02e1-22ad-4bce-8bc4-24affaa9cbdd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe7f9119-71e5-44e9-8903-d7f459fba876",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *If an app only shows songs from the Top 10, what happens to everyone else?*\n- *What rule might that filter use?*\n- *What is one change you would make to the filter to make the playlist feel more fair or inclusive?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students notice that a simple rule, such as filtering by high popularity, hides many other songs and artists. Listen for comments about lesser-known, new, indie, or local artists being invisible, and gently connect this to the idea that “what you see” is controlled by hidden conditions. Surface the idea that fairness comes from the conditions we choose. Highlight suggestions that change numeric thresholds, add extra conditions, or create multiple views, and emphasize that these are all choices about what conditions to use.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Wrap up the warm up by stating explicitly: every filter starts as a condition, and every condition is a choice that affects what people see and do not see. Optionally, if you have one extra minute, invite students to jot a quick pseudocode condition such as `if artist.popularity > 90` in their notebook.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students write pseudocode, remind them it does not need perfect syntax. Focus them on the idea that a one-line rule can have a big impact on who gets included or excluded."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fe7f9119-71e5-44e9-8903-d7f459fba876",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d78f02e1-22ad-4bce-8bc4-24affaa9cbdd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d267a3df-2bdf-481b-a47f-4b9b1d863fad",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d267a3df-2bdf-481b-a47f-4b9b1d863fad",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d78f02e1-22ad-4bce-8bc4-24affaa9cbdd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1a369e79-53bb-4f35-96ce-356b52c6b2c7",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1a369e79-53bb-4f35-96ce-356b52c6b2c7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d78f02e1-22ad-4bce-8bc4-24affaa9cbdd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c5b5dea0-816a-4bca-9fa8-c368a7c30a28",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Discover how different filter patterns impact results, inclusivity, and reliability.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly introduce the ECHO framework (Explore → Co-Create → Highlight → Own). Explain that in this first activity, students will explore two AI-generated filter patterns that use .map() and conditions to return only pop songs, then compare what makes each pattern strong."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c5b5dea0-816a-4bca-9fa8-c368a7c30a28",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1253fc59-4ddb-45a2-a9be-b2bb95608e04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d4e2ec8d-a03b-4fe1-9c70-0a2e4cd2e497",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this part of the lesson, AI is your code coach. You will not ask it to build the whole app—only the filter functions. Your job is to understand the patterns and what makes them fair and reliable.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "ECHO is a framework that students will use throughout the rest of the unit. It is helpful for learning new content and wanting to examine multiple ways of implementing a new programming structure or concept, before independently writing code."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d4e2ec8d-a03b-4fe1-9c70-0a2e4cd2e497",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1253fc59-4ddb-45a2-a9be-b2bb95608e04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "70859052-e291-4434-b0e9-12bd62871d62",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 8, Level 1.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who are asking AI for just the filter functions, not a full app rewrite. Check that:\n- `filterGenreVersionA` builds and returns a new array of only sci-fi movies using a condition like `if (movie.genre === \"Sci-Fi\")`.\n- `filterGenreVersionB` uses `.map()` plus a cleanup step (such as `.filter(Boolean)`) so there are no undefined entries.\n- Students can explain one structural difference between Version A and B and identify a strong practice related to missing values or clean output.",
        "progression_name": "ECHO Framework: Explore and Own Filter Logic Patterns",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students get stuck, suggest specific follow-ups to ask AI, such as \"What happens if a movie is missing a genre field?\" or \"Can you adjust this to work with my movies array?\" Emphasize that they should read AI's explanations, not just paste code."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "70859052-e291-4434-b0e9-12bd62871d62",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1253fc59-4ddb-45a2-a9be-b2bb95608e04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6bf2ee38-f708-479d-af41-b52c42dcd920",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6bf2ee38-f708-479d-af41-b52c42dcd920",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1253fc59-4ddb-45a2-a9be-b2bb95608e04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c2fe8d43-698b-4d80-9290-8f3c806b54c0",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What is one important difference you noticed between Version A and Version B? Which strong practice do you think matters most for fairness or reliability?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students connect code structure to fairness and reliability. Listen for ideas like \"this version starts with an empty array,\" \"this one skips songs without a genre instead of crashing,\" or \"filtering out undefined keeps the results clean,\" and link those to clearer, more trustworthy filters.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define filter.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to walk through the parts of a generalized filter function.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the CHAT Framework.\n- Clarify: Decompose all the steps necessary to create the desired output.\n- Hone: Draft a prompt that will add one of the decomposed features.\n- Apply: Run and test the code with real data.\n- Transfer: Turn your best prompt into a reusable template.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "CHAT is a framework that students will use throughout the course. It is helpful for decomposing a larger problem into smaller steps, then creating targeted and focused prompts to implement one feature at a time. Once applied and tested, students will abstract a general reusable prompt template that they can reuse in future tasks. They will save this prompt into the backpack."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c2fe8d43-698b-4d80-9290-8f3c806b54c0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1253fc59-4ddb-45a2-a9be-b2bb95608e04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c7e47030-7032-4752-9771-135201b235e5",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c7e47030-7032-4752-9771-135201b235e5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1253fc59-4ddb-45a2-a9be-b2bb95608e04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b2ce3163-35ad-47ff-a621-996269a342b0",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 4.\n- Level 2: Students complete a choice level and apply the CHAT Framework.\n- Level 3: Review the definition for _filter_.\n- Level 4: Explain _filter_ in their own words.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work:\n- Check that each student has clearly Clarified the goal of their filter in words before diving into code.\n- Encourage them to Hone one condition at a time and ask AI to explain any new lines, especially guard clauses for missing or undefined data.\n- Watch that their apps Apply filters correctly (UI updates and accurate result counts) and that they Transfer what they learned by editing their final prompt and recording how they improved fairness.",
        "progression_name": "Filter Choice Levels"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b2ce3163-35ad-47ff-a621-996269a342b0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1253fc59-4ddb-45a2-a9be-b2bb95608e04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "48cffa3f-1962-4e77-a577-919383578d5d",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Filter",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students get stuck debugging, have them use AI as a validator by asking, “Suggest test data for missing or undefined fields” or “Can you show what happens if some items are missing this property?” This keeps the focus on testing and fairness, not on generating fancy code."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "48cffa3f-1962-4e77-a577-919383578d5d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1253fc59-4ddb-45a2-a9be-b2bb95608e04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7d993af3-1cec-47cb-a723-d7261f13015e",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7d993af3-1cec-47cb-a723-d7261f13015e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1253fc59-4ddb-45a2-a9be-b2bb95608e04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "57748801-b3b1-474d-8859-2f6efe3b8e2f",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What condition in your filter made the biggest difference to fairness? How did AI help you test or improve that condition?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students connect their implementation details—like guard clauses, lowercasing, or multi-condition checks—to real impacts on fairness and reliability. Encourage them to name concrete examples (for instance, “skipping songs with missing popularity so the app does not crash” or “treating 'Tomato' and 'tomato' the same”) and to see AI as a partner in generating tests and spotting gaps.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, invite students to share one fairness-focused test case they used (for example, missing color field, missing genre, or no matches). You can collect a few on the board as a class-generated checklist for testing filters in future lessons. Highlight that good fairness test cases often involve missing, extreme, or unexpected data. Encourage students to reuse and expand this list whenever they design new filters."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "57748801-b3b1-474d-8859-2f6efe3b8e2f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1253fc59-4ddb-45a2-a9be-b2bb95608e04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "060cc85d-531c-4194-a5cb-a3d4a950dc09",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student an index card or piece of paper.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to use one of the question stems to write a question they have from the lesson that day. Give them time to share their questions with the person next to them. Invite 1–2 volunteers to share their examples with the whole class.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "You may choose to collect these questions and use them as a gauge for where understanding may still need strengthening in your class."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "060cc85d-531c-4194-a5cb-a3d4a950dc09",
        "lesson_activity.key": "191ae1dd-ad16-4b19-9d96-dac9600b8de3"
      }
    },
    {
