      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4673c3d4-193d-413c-b98d-3453fd240cea",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Surface how tiny details affect search results and build intuition for edge cases.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to silently predict what results will return when someone searches for the term that appears in the search box.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students jump into coding explanations, gently redirect them to describe what they see happening in the results before talking about how the code works."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4673c3d4-193d-413c-b98d-3453fd240cea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e2a1ded-b732-43f7-af9e-64189c12394d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7c7450e9-f57c-4165-b04a-4490202d0881",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7c7450e9-f57c-4165-b04a-4490202d0881",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e2a1ded-b732-43f7-af9e-64189c12394d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ef8b8611-45e3-4e47-8f23-87524ee3a768",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to predict which of the cards fit the criteria for a search for the term \"Pop\" in the Title property.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Look closely at 'PoPcorN'. It has the letters P, o, and p. Why exactly did the computer reject it when we searched for 'Pop'?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Highlight that an exact match is required: 'P' is not equal to 'p'. We often think computers are smart, but in this case, they are extremely literal. A computer doesn't read the word \"Pop\"; it reads a specific code for \"Big P\", \"Little o\", \"Little p\". If the sequence doesn't match perfectly—like in Popcorn where the third letter is a \"Big P\"—the computer says \"No match!\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to predict which of the cards fit the criteria for a search for the term \"pop\" in the Title property\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Does this search return match your expectations? Why or why not?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that users don't usually pay attention to capitalization when typing. If a user types \"pop\" and gets zero results for \"K-Pop\" or \"Pop\", they will think the app is broken or the song doesn't exist.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to predict which of the cards, if any, fit the criteria when the search box is left empty.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Does it make logical sense for a computer to show ALL results when the search bar is empty?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Every string technically \"contains\" an empty string. When you first open a search page or clear the search bar, you usually expect to see a default list of all items, not a blank screen. The \"empty string = all\" logic is actually a helpful feature in many applications. This looks like a bug, but it's often a feature.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ef8b8611-45e3-4e47-8f23-87524ee3a768",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e2a1ded-b732-43f7-af9e-64189c12394d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "44455452-4cf5-4240-80ac-f82a573172d5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Examine a dataset and imagine useful searches and filters before writing any code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _query_.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Searching activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to quietly scan the dataset. Have them circle or highlight fields that repeat (for example, same category).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ask probing questions like: What kind of value is this field? Could someone want to search or filter by it? Help students see patterns (like multiple items sharing the same category).",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Check whether students can correctly identify different field types and repeated values; this will matter for choosing search criteria later."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "44455452-4cf5-4240-80ac-f82a573172d5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d966b8a1-d95b-4f90-b321-4bda5903c4cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d0b3aa9b-05f9-4d4f-8a10-7f85e7e7cfa0",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d0b3aa9b-05f9-4d4f-8a10-7f85e7e7cfa0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d966b8a1-d95b-4f90-b321-4bda5903c4cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ce5ac99-16c3-464e-b038-f6d468e9a507",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students work individually or in small groups to propose at least two different filters or searches a user might want to run on this dataset for an item of the student's choosing. For each filter, they should note which field it uses and describe how it would narrow or sort the list.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ask questions like: Is this filter something a real user would care about? How would the app decide which items match? Encourage them to think about fairness (for example, not hiding items unexpectedly) and clarity.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle, suggest one filter from a numeric field (for example, year or rating) and one from a categorical field (for example, genre or club type) to get them started."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ce5ac99-16c3-464e-b038-f6d468e9a507",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d966b8a1-d95b-4f90-b321-4bda5903c4cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ce552ba4-a3f9-403f-bd48-2be112313cac",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which filters from your list seem most useful to real users, and what is one edge case you definitely want your future search function to handle?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface a few high-value filters (for example, by category, rating, or tags) and highlight common edge cases (case differences, empty queries, missing data). Capture these on the board as “rules of thumb” to guide coding later.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 9, Level 1. Students will:\n- Run the app and test searches like blue, Blue, blu, and an empty query.\n- Paste the `searchColors` function into AI and ask focused questions (why lowercase, what happens on empty query, does it change the original list?).\n- Add short comments above the function explaining the rules they discovered.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are actually reading AI explanations and translating them into their own words in comments. Ask: How is this function handling case differences? What happens when the query is \"\"? Are we ever changing the original colors array?",
        "progression_name": "Explore and Explain",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students try to rewrite or \"improve\" the function, remind them this level is about understanding and documenting logic, not changing it. Emphasize case-insensitivity, empty-query behavior, and returning a new results array."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ce552ba4-a3f9-403f-bd48-2be112313cac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d966b8a1-d95b-4f90-b321-4bda5903c4cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6cd42602-86fa-4b7f-b4aa-c6f6ee780db4",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6cd42602-86fa-4b7f-b4aa-c6f6ee780db4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d966b8a1-d95b-4f90-b321-4bda5903c4cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c40b7dc4-8b15-43e2-b4b3-5379ed65cf6c",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How did the application handle capitalization differences?*\n- *What rule does it follow when the query is empty?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Make sure students can clearly state that `.toLowerCase()` makes the search case-insensitive, that an empty string acts as a reset/show-all condition, and that the function uses a new results array instead of changing data. These ideas will be reused in their own functions.\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Explore working search logic and then co-build your own text, dropdown, or chip-based search with AI.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the three search patterns: text field, dropdown, and chip/tag-based search. Briefly connect each pattern to the filters students just designed (for example, text for flexible matching, dropdown for fixed categories, chips for tags)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c40b7dc4-8b15-43e2-b4b3-5379ed65cf6c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d966b8a1-d95b-4f90-b321-4bda5903c4cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ea1467cf-8da2-4828-9b2a-0f85eaecab4d",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "These are common patterns in real web apps. You will analyze one working example, learn the rules behind it, then choose one pattern to co-build with AI.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ea1467cf-8da2-4828-9b2a-0f85eaecab4d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d966b8a1-d95b-4f90-b321-4bda5903c4cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1c903373-5f43-4b18-b7c6-dda18b478f11",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 9, Level 2. Explain that students will now choose one search style to co-build with AI: Option A (text field), Option B (dropdown), or Option C (chips/tags). Clarify that each option uses the same core ideas about queries and safe search, just in different UIs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Help students choose an option that fits their comfort and interest. Ensure every student knows which level letter (a, b, or c) they are working on.",
        "progression_name": "Co-Build a Search"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1c903373-5f43-4b18-b7c6-dda18b478f11",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d966b8a1-d95b-4f90-b321-4bda5903c4cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "457e7a9a-403d-4455-bace-40e29012df67",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Query",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For students who need more structure, suggest Option B (dropdown). For students excited about arrays and tags, suggest Option C."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "457e7a9a-403d-4455-bace-40e29012df67",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d966b8a1-d95b-4f90-b321-4bda5903c4cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d4cc7be7-7ad4-4773-b0f8-634fcb5ab0c1",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d4cc7be7-7ad4-4773-b0f8-634fcb5ab0c1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d966b8a1-d95b-4f90-b321-4bda5903c4cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5102a2dd-923a-47bb-95c2-a504c5dd4b00",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What rules did your search function follow to stay fair and predictable?*\n- *How did working with AI (CHAT steps) help you design and debug your search?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Draw out shared rules like case-insensitivity, clear behavior for empty queries, not mutating original data, and guarding missing fields. Highlight AI as a partner for clarifying requirements and checking edge cases, not just generating code."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5102a2dd-923a-47bb-95c2-a504c5dd4b00",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d966b8a1-d95b-4f90-b321-4bda5903c4cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "16b2fae0-743c-437c-a5ce-f944b2c10f97",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What is one improvement you would still like to make to your search function?*\n- *How could AI help you design good tests for that change?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Encourage students to see search as something they can iterate on and to view AI as a tool for generating and refining edge-case tests, not just code. Listen for ideas like adding more filters, improving messages, or handling more tricky inputs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "16b2fae0-743c-437c-a5ce-f944b2c10f97",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4e815a1d-1864-418b-a8fa-c7436e1a4f4d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "72cc2999-3e93-4587-92cc-db22a1d73952",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 5 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "72cc2999-3e93-4587-92cc-db22a1d73952",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4e815a1d-1864-418b-a8fa-c7436e1a4f4d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "49419e42-f18b-4adf-9f93-e866144dfca3",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Notice how different sort rules change what appears first and who gets seen.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show three versions of the same feed (Most Popular, Alphabetical, Newest). Give students a moment with each view to jot down which items they see first.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which items jump to the top in each view? Who benefits and who might be hidden? Which view feels most trustworthy, and why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that the same content can look very different depending on the sort rule. Guide them to notice that certain apps/creators benefit from each rule, and that “Most Popular” might favor already-famous items, while “Newest” favors recency. Emphasize that no sort is neutral; each sends a message about what matters.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Highlight that sorting rules shape visibility and trust. Connect the app store/feed example to the idea that we can design different sorting pipelines, and those choices have fairness impacts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "49419e42-f18b-4adf-9f93-e866144dfca3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aba0ece5-2d28-4511-a344-8b4dbb48f2b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dcc553b9-b629-471d-8073-713a79ddd77e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Use a concrete card set to explore how the order of filter and sort affects efficiency.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly introduce the MyTube card activity. Explain that students will build two different sequences (filter → sort and sort → filter) and then compare them for effort and fairness.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 10, Level 1. Walk through the instructions for Step One and Two. Emphasize using only the visible Sports tag for filtering and the exact scoring rule score = likes − dislikes + subscribers for ranking. Remind students not to add or change tags unless you explicitly say so.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are correctly applying the filter first, then sorting only the remaining cards. Ask: How are you deciding which card has the highest score? Did any videos that look like sports disappear because they lacked the Sports tag? Prompt them to think about missing or misapplied labels.",
        "progression_name": "Introduction to a Filter & Sort Pipeline",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "You may choose to have students complete this activity in an unplugged fashion using this Intro to Filter & Sort - Activity Guide version of the same activity contained in Level 1. If students struggle with the score, have them compute it for just two or three cards together, writing scores on sticky notes before ranking."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dcc553b9-b629-471d-8073-713a79ddd77e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "90147e5c-27a0-431f-962a-ff4b9d993df6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ccae9d4e-a465-4b6a-a140-eb1f549b511e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ccae9d4e-a465-4b6a-a140-eb1f549b511e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "90147e5c-27a0-431f-962a-ff4b9d993df6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "33c988ad-147c-4a4c-8d42-684637a43c47",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Which algorithm felt better overall: filter → sort or sort → filter?*\n- *Why, thinking about both effort and fairness?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface that filter → sort usually takes less work because sorting a smaller set is “lighter,” and that both sequences can still hide mis-labeled items. Steer students to notice that good pipelines think about both efficiency and fairness, not just one.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _pipeline_.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students of the CHAT framework. Point out to them that they should treat this framework as dynamic rather than linear and that the middle steps should be repeated and iterated on as many times as needed.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "CHAT is a framework that students will use throughout the course. It is helpful for decomposing a larger problem into smaller steps, then creating targeted and focused prompts to implement one feature at a time. Once applied and tested, students will abstract a general reusable prompt template that they can reuse in future tasks. They will save this prompt into the backpack."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "33c988ad-147c-4a4c-8d42-684637a43c47",
        "lesson_activity.key": "90147e5c-27a0-431f-962a-ff4b9d993df6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dac66f9a-aace-4265-8dda-512480328feb",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 5.\n- Level 2: Students pick a scenario (For You Page, Comment Climb, or Ad Transparency), use CHAT to design a filter → sort → map _pipeline_, implement it in Web Lab, and build an AI-supported test table.\n- Level 3: Students write a free-response reflection about how their _pipeline_’s sort and map choices affect who gets seen and how fair it feels.\n- Level 4: Read a definition, example, and visual description of a _pipeline_.\n- Level 5: Write their own explanation of _pipeline_ with an example.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Help students choose an option that interests them and fits their skill level. As they work, check that their plain-language CHAT planning matches the code they write and that their test tables include tricky edge cases (missing labels, low engagement, borderline ages, bot bursts, etc.). Ask: How could this rule unintentionally hide or over-promote certain people or content?",
        "progression_name": "Design a Filter → Sort → Render Pipeline"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dac66f9a-aace-4265-8dda-512480328feb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "90147e5c-27a0-431f-962a-ff4b9d993df6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "016e6780-61d6-4d69-b74a-728f4d34ebf4",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Free Response: Pipeline Fairness"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "016e6780-61d6-4d69-b74a-728f4d34ebf4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "90147e5c-27a0-431f-962a-ff4b9d993df6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "41de2a94-98b9-4aef-a2e5-3d4769720aec",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Pipeline",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time is tight, allow pairs to collaborate on one pipeline but have each student complete their own reflection on Level 3 so you can assess individual understanding."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "41de2a94-98b9-4aef-a2e5-3d4769720aec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "90147e5c-27a0-431f-962a-ff4b9d993df6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df8d94de-f6b5-40ec-9244-6418028867c5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to write a brief exit reflection (on paper or digitally) naming one concrete improvement they would make to their pipeline for fairness or representation.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df8d94de-f6b5-40ec-9244-6418028867c5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "32e501d2-8d94-4e5e-8de5-90fdd2e87485"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "13e9a360-744c-44d9-a689-606aec312e9a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 6 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "13e9a360-744c-44d9-a689-606aec312e9a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "32e501d2-8d94-4e5e-8de5-90fdd2e87485"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7096f3ea-3334-453d-a061-05a0d7a15b73",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What's an app or website that helps you explore or compare information?*\n- *What makes it helpful, fair, or easy to use?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify qualities of apps that present data clearly and fairly, grounding today’s work in what makes information accessible and meaningful.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7096f3ea-3334-453d-a061-05a0d7a15b73",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e2f080bb-9f60-47c3-8084-ea9d565b0780"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bf398fa0-9f23-4284-bb75-7dd24b43f72c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Values Explorer Project Planning Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the first part of the project requirements.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to read the rubric on the last two pages of the project planning guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you notice about the project requirements and rubric? What does success look like for this project?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students share what they notice about the project requirements and rubric and identify the components to implement in their program.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the milestone structure of the project. Explain that the Values Explorer Project is divided into four milestones, each with its own goals and deliverables. Use the visual roadmap to preview the journey students will take — from defining their app concept to showcasing their final product.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that milestones help them manage time and track progress, just like in real design and development projects. Encourage them to think of each milestone as a \"checkpoint\" to reflect on what's working, what needs improvement, and how their design aligns with their user and purpose."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bf398fa0-9f23-4284-bb75-7dd24b43f72c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e2f080bb-9f60-47c3-8084-ea9d565b0780"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d7101820-cedb-4599-825b-f8845eb21dae",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d7101820-cedb-4599-825b-f8845eb21dae",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e2f080bb-9f60-47c3-8084-ea9d565b0780"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "27e4513e-e069-4d5c-8be0-11b767f41d85",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that today's focus is Milestone 1: Plan Your Values Explorer & Design The Schema, where students identify a central value, design their schema, and generate a prototype dataset. Direct students to open their Values Explorer Project Guide and locate the Milestone 1 section. Have them read through the Goals and Deliverables so they understand what needs to be completed by the end of this milestone.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to highlight or note parts of the deliverables that feel most exciting or challenging. This helps you gauge engagement and plan support for different groups."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "27e4513e-e069-4d5c-8be0-11b767f41d85",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e2f080bb-9f60-47c3-8084-ea9d565b0780"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "77e77da6-deba-4f92-91ac-cf7ad99d6f03",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "77e77da6-deba-4f92-91ac-cf7ad99d6f03",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e2f080bb-9f60-47c3-8084-ea9d565b0780"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5e7ce93d-e1ac-4130-8f94-cbabd7135667",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct student to Level 1 to begin Milestone 1: Plan Your Values Explorer & Design The Schema. Students can choose which tool best supports their current task: brainstorming ideas, organizing their plan, or sketching out their schema plan, and generating their prototype dataset.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students explore the hub, ask guiding questions such as:\n- “Which value does your project focus on?”\n- “What kinds of items will be in your dataset?”\n- “How does your schema help represent what matters to users?”\n- “Who might be missing from your data right now?”\n\nUse these check-ins to confirm students are moving from idea to planning, rather than jumping straight into code.",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 1",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to experiment with multiple tools as they develop their plan — for example, brainstorming with the Idea Bot before designing their schema or chatting with Schema Chat before building out the prototype dataset. Remind them that testing and refining their ideas here will save time later once they start coding.\n\nSet expectations for pacing — Milestone 1 can typically be finished by the end of today's class or early next class. Encourage students to focus first on clarity of idea rather than polish."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5e7ce93d-e1ac-4130-8f94-cbabd7135667",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e2f080bb-9f60-47c3-8084-ea9d565b0780"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "45cb95bb-83a7-4762-8f99-397c70f8757a",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Milestone 1 Reflection section in their project planning guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are discussing and sharing these reflection questions with a thought partner.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits, invite a few volunteers to share aloud or conduct a quick pair-and-share to close."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "45cb95bb-83a7-4762-8f99-397c70f8757a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e2f080bb-9f60-47c3-8084-ea9d565b0780"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bfa23303-e72c-4d3e-a945-7ce7d4b4cd9f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts:\n- *What's one part of your dataset that you're most excited to display visually?*\n- *What's one thing you're not sure how to show clearly yet?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on data display challenges, preparing for wireframing and UI design decisions.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bfa23303-e72c-4d3e-a945-7ce7d4b4cd9f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a93a9603-521f-4a95-9a89-5b1a32d270c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c37cc441-d800-4f82-92c3-aea39f661f42",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks to complete for Day 2 of the project. Explain that today students will focus on completing Milestone 1 and starting work on Milestone 2. Direct students to open their Values Explorer Project Guide and locate the Milestone 1 and 2 sections. Have them review the Goals and Deliverables for each milestone so they understand what to prioritize during work time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to use Level 1 if they still need to complete any Milestone 1 work or would benefit from one of the support tools, and move to Level 2 to begin Milestone 2, where they will shift from planning their data to designing how it will appear on the page. Explain that today’s focus is creating clear wireframes and starting the UI structure that will eventually display their dataset using `.map()`. Emphasize that students should aim for clarity rather than polish—simple layouts that show where cards will go and how each schema field will be labeled. Encourage students to begin with one clean card layout and build from there, mirroring how developers prototype simple, testable pieces before adding complexity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are on task and progressing toward a complete first draft. Ask check-in questions like:\n- “Which schema fields will appear on your card?”\n- “Where will users look first when viewing your data?”\n- “Does your wireframe show clear labels for every field?”\n- “How will you make your data easy to scan or compare?”\n\nProvide quick support if students get stuck — redirect them to the AI Chat or Project Support Bot for troubleshooting help before intervening directly.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 2"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c37cc441-d800-4f82-92c3-aea39f661f42",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a93a9603-521f-4a95-9a89-5b1a32d270c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "54d3f01c-e24c-4f3f-b93d-4b76f3c1a7e4",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Milestone 2 Reflection section in their project planning guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students write and discuss, listen for reflections that describe how their page layout and card design help users understand the data clearly. Encourage students to notice whether their labels, headings, and visual structure make the dataset easy to scan, and to capture specific ideas about what they might refine next time.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "progression_name": "Milestone Two",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits, invite a few students to share one design choice that makes their data easier to understand. Highlight that noticing how layout supports clarity is an important design skill — it shows they’re thinking about how real users will read and interpret the information in their app."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "54d3f01c-e24c-4f3f-b93d-4b76f3c1a7e4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a93a9603-521f-4a95-9a89-5b1a32d270c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93b8973f-5a47-44ac-97e6-0901c70bddbe",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What’s something in your app that isn’t showing the data the way you expected? What do you think might be causing it?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students practice articulating how their code transforms or displays data, normalizing debugging as an ongoing part of algorithm design.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93b8973f-5a47-44ac-97e6-0901c70bddbe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fd2e37e5-bcb1-4bcd-9d24-6910ef9e85a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8124f059-9edf-4cd1-a589-5a125fe83a30",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks to complete for Day 3 of the project. Explain that today students will focus on Milestones 2 and 3. Direct students to open their Values Explorer Project Guide and locate the Milestone 2 and 3 sections. Have them review the Goals and Deliverables for each milestone so they understand what to prioritize during work time.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 3",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Point out that these milestones move from shaping how data is displayed to making that data interactive. Encourage students not to aim for perfection at first — focus on getting one feature or transformation working clearly, and then improve it through testing, debugging, and fairness checks."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8124f059-9edf-4cd1-a589-5a125fe83a30",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fd2e37e5-bcb1-4bcd-9d24-6910ef9e85a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9f515bdc-60f3-4fea-a8f0-8f89bf90b30f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9f515bdc-60f3-4fea-a8f0-8f89bf90b30f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fd2e37e5-bcb1-4bcd-9d24-6910ef9e85a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f709910b-934b-40c6-8d85-6bdc42e5f787",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the TAG Peer Review process and model what thoughtful feedback looks like. Have students pair up or form small groups and TAG one to two peers' projects. They can leave feedback on sticky notes (digital or paper) directly at their peer's workspace or comment within the project guide if using shared docs. Emphasize that this is not a final critique — it's a chance to gather early insights as part of Milestone 3.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be at different points when it's time for peer review. Reassure them that work doesn't need to be complete to be reviewed — feedback during the process helps improve the final product.\nReinforce the mindset that design and development are iterative, and peer feedback offers new perspectives and guidance.\nIf time allows, invite a few students to share one piece of feedback they received that will guide their next steps."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f709910b-934b-40c6-8d85-6bdc42e5f787",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fd2e37e5-bcb1-4bcd-9d24-6910ef9e85a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f7591866-1962-4cab-84bc-7ccc77913a62",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Milestone 3 Reflection section in their project planning guide. Let students know they can continue to add on to this reflection as they continue to make progress on Milestone 3 and conduct more testing.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, invite a few students to share how they’ve been testing their app or what they plan to test next. Highlight that catching fairness issues or algorithmic quirks early is a sign of strong computational thinking — developers constantly refine how data is displayed to ensure experiences are both accurate and equitable."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f7591866-1962-4cab-84bc-7ccc77913a62",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fd2e37e5-bcb1-4bcd-9d24-6910ef9e85a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "74748242-d9a5-433c-8023-e19adbbe0e0b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If someone were testing your app right now, what’s the one thing you’d want them to notice first?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on the purpose and user experience of their app, setting an intentional focus for peer testing and feedback during today’s class.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "74748242-d9a5-433c-8023-e19adbbe0e0b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c82351c-8cb3-4bf0-91d3-fd086ae57bf9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0a2ffb52-16de-4c97-b3f3-885115195c37",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks to complete for Day 4 of the project. Explain that today students will focus on Milestones 3 and 4. Direct students to open their Values Explorer Project Guide and locate the Milestone 3 and 4 sections. Have them review the Goals and Deliverables for each milestone so they understand what to prioritize during work time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the TAG Peer Review process and model what thoughtful feedback looks like. Have students pair up or form small groups and TAG one to two peers' projects. They can leave feedback on sticky notes (digital or paper) directly at their peer's workspace or comment within the project guide if using shared docs. Emphasize that this is not a final critique — it's a chance to gather early insights as part of Milestone 3.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 4",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be at different points when it's time for peer review. Reassure them that work doesn't need to be complete to be reviewed — feedback during the process helps improve the final product.\nReinforce the mindset that design and development are iterative, and peer feedback offers new perspectives and guidance.\nIf time allows, invite a few students to share one piece of feedback they received that will guide their next steps."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0a2ffb52-16de-4c97-b3f3-885115195c37",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c82351c-8cb3-4bf0-91d3-fd086ae57bf9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c784e809-6907-4c59-b06e-835ac48d37a5",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to revisit the Milestone 3 Reflection section in their project planning guide and add on to what they wrote the previous class period. They should update their Fixes + Features List, record any new debugging insights, and reflect on how their search/filter/sort choices influence which items users see first.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "When students revisit their reflections today, point out that adding new insights after another round of testing is exactly how professional engineers document progress. Encourage them to notice what changed, what improved, and what new questions emerged—this shows they’re thinking like real developers who learn from every test cycle to make smarter, more informed decisions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c784e809-6907-4c59-b06e-835ac48d37a5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c82351c-8cb3-4bf0-91d3-fd086ae57bf9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "40d33a22-b0c5-42f7-b696-55c13b0ee370",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What’s one thing you still need to check or polish before your app feels ready to share?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify final improvement tasks related to clarity, accessibility, fairness, or data accuracy as they prepare to finalize their Values Explorer app.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up",
        "progression_name": "Milestone Three"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "40d33a22-b0c5-42f7-b696-55c13b0ee370",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6be8d3a5-f0a3-4a3f-99d9-bb1d45ef5176"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5fa2271d-433e-45c0-80a9-d0cb322db933",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks to complete for Day 5 of the project. Explain that today is focused on Milestone 4 – Finalize, where students polish their app and make it ready for showcasing. Remind students to check their Values Explorer Project Guide for the Milestone 4 deliverables.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to the last two pages of their Values Explorer Project Guide, where the project rubric is located. Have students review each category carefully and mark where they believe their project currently falls. Encourage honesty — the goal is to identify strengths and areas for continued growth as intentional designers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are engaging thoughtfully with each rubric criterion, not rushing to completion.",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone Four",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that self-assessment isn't just about scoring their project — it's a professional habit that helps them take ownership of their design choices. Say something like: \"Designers constantly evaluate their own work to make sure it aligns with their goals, audience, and purpose. This rubric helps you see where you've grown and what you can still refine.\"\n\nIf time allows, have students highlight one category they feel especially proud of and one they'd like to improve next time."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5fa2271d-433e-45c0-80a9-d0cb322db933",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6be8d3a5-f0a3-4a3f-99d9-bb1d45ef5176"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5b856316-07f0-465b-b72d-338a4467c0f5",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5b856316-07f0-465b-b72d-338a4467c0f5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6be8d3a5-f0a3-4a3f-99d9-bb1d45ef5176"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "859e2c24-86b0-4877-9571-8b86e16719ed",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 4 to finish developing their Values Explorer Projects. Explain that this is their final dedicated work session for Milestone 4 – Finalize. Reinforce that today's goal is to prioritize improvements based on their self-assessment and TAG peer feedback, rather than trying to fix everything at once. Instruct students to use the feedback they received from their TAG review and self-assessment to finalize their Values Explorer Project. Have students submit their projects at the end of the allotted time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are on task. Remind students to comment their code with meaningful descriptions. Have students review their rubric and prioritize work on any rows where they do not yet show extensive evidence.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that professional designers always prioritize fixes before a release. Say something like: \"You don't need to fix everything today — focus on what will have the biggest impact for your user. Iteration is about making smart choices with the time you have.\"\nIf time allows at the end, invite students to do a brief pair preview, where they open a peer's site and give one final piece of positive or constructive feedback."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "859e2c24-86b0-4877-9571-8b86e16719ed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6be8d3a5-f0a3-4a3f-99d9-bb1d45ef5176"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6eb13b53-3570-4829-97d4-f576bf860b8e",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6eb13b53-3570-4829-97d4-f576bf860b8e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6be8d3a5-f0a3-4a3f-99d9-bb1d45ef5176"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6d701d1b-592c-48fc-9921-9d2f9283bbbc",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Distribute several sticky notes to each student for the gallery walk.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students begin the Gallery Walk by displaying their Values Explorer Apps on their computers, then moving around the room to explore classmates' projects. As they visit each one, students should use their sticky notes to leave feedback, kudos, or questions following the TAG approach (Tell, Ask, Give). Encourage them to engage with a range of projects—not just those of close friends.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Move around the room to observe how students are engaging with the displayed projects. Encourage students to leave a note about what they find interesting or insightful about their peers' work. Ensure that students are thoughtfully engaging with a variety of projects, not just those of their closest peers.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Frame the Gallery Walk as a celebration of learning and creativity, not a critique. Say something like: \"This is your chance to see how everyone's ideas came to life in different ways. You might even get inspired by someone else's approach!\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6d701d1b-592c-48fc-9921-9d2f9283bbbc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6be8d3a5-f0a3-4a3f-99d9-bb1d45ef5176"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "176f8c8d-0f88-4952-832b-c0a0a581b4f5",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "176f8c8d-0f88-4952-832b-c0a0a581b4f5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6be8d3a5-f0a3-4a3f-99d9-bb1d45ef5176"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ec6d85e0-36e8-45d5-afc8-5221957abc05",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ve gone from choosing a value and designing a schema to building, debugging, refining, and evaluating your app. This final reflection helps you capture what you learned — not only about coding and UI design, but about representing data fairly and using AI responsibly.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ec6d85e0-36e8-45d5-afc8-5221957abc05",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6be8d3a5-f0a3-4a3f-99d9-bb1d45ef5176"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "68d224d3-db39-4059-bbcf-c4f8aed90143",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Final Reflection and Final Self-Check sections in their Values Explorer Project Guide. Explain that this final step helps them consolidate their learning and ensure their site meets key design goals — usability, consistency, accessibility, and thoughtful use of AI.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Encourage students to elaborate on their answers with specific examples of how they used feedback or iteration to improve their project. If students finish early, prompt them to help peers finalize their submissions or review their rubric one more time for completeness.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "You can use the Values Explorer Planning Guide and Final Reflection as evidence for the Process, Iteration, and Code/UI Quality rubric criteria. The Final Reflection can be used as evidence for the Ethical Reasoning rubric criteria."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "68d224d3-db39-4059-bbcf-c4f8aed90143",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6be8d3a5-f0a3-4a3f-99d9-bb1d45ef5176"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eed2fdd5-529e-4032-9cc4-932850eda454",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eed2fdd5-529e-4032-9cc4-932850eda454",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6be8d3a5-f0a3-4a3f-99d9-bb1d45ef5176"
      }
    },
